{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MT9LbUBWCBZ24S7tGQuMpx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'CIVO is a general architectural vulnerability in imagination-based\n2\t  actor-critic with dt-conditioning. Under best tier, the gradient decomposition (93%\n3\t  obs decoder → dt_emb, 7% KL, 0% reward) + Full-SGS (exp_018, NVE=1.00x→0.72x) closes\n4\t  the mechanism at gradient level. Cross-3-environment replication (Walker Walk ✓,\n5\t  Cheetah Run, PickCube/Cartpole) elevates the finding from DreamerV3-specific to\n6\t  structural warning for temporal-abstraction-in-world-models. TAWM contrast (MPC\n7\t  succeeds, imagination fails) is the headline novelty. The 1.5x NVE threshold maintains\n8\t  ≥3/4 separating power in every new environment. Pathway B (008e_v3_s2-type eval-driven\n9\t  collapse) is either rare (≤1/4 cross-env) or gets clean mechanistic characterization\n10\t  via control experiment. Missing from current plan: Full-SGS replication in Cheetah/Cartpole\n11\t  — needed to close \"gradient mechanism generalizes\" claim per tier spec.\n12\t\n13\t  '\n14\timagined_experiments:\n15\t- name: Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\n16\t  plan_id: plan_011\n17\t  description: 'Replicate CIVO collapse with 2 additional seeds. Registry already\n18\t    has data: 009e (seed 1) collapsed NVE peak 4.12x; 009b (seed 2) self-corrected\n19\t    NVE peak 2.05x. Under best tier, both seeds exceed NVE>1.5x (confirmed), collapse\n20\t    is stochastic 1/2 (confirmed).\n21\t\n22\t    '\n23\t  expected_results:\n24\t    seed1_eval_105K: 131\n25\t    seed1_nve_peak: 4.12\n26\t    seed1_nve_peak_step: 55000\n27\t    seed1_collapse: true\n28\t    seed1_collapse_step: 55000\n29\t    seed2_eval_105K: 268\n30\t    seed2_nve_peak: 2.05\n31\t    seed2_nve_peak_step: 25000\n32\t    seed2_collapse: false\n33\t    seed2_self_correction_nve_105K: 0.38\n34\t    both_seeds_exceed_1.5x: true\n35\t    threshold_separating_power: 2/2 exceed 1.5x at 35K+\n36\t    collapse_rate: 1/2 (stochastic)\n37\t  result_rationale: 'Trained dt_emb creates systematic overestimation because the\n38\t    RSSM fidelity gap at dt=2 (obs gap 1.30x at inflated absolute levels, reward gap\n39\t    2.78x) is amplified through the observation decoder→dt_emb gradient pathway (93%\n40\t    of dt_emb gradient). Every seed with trained dt_emb reliably enters the pathological\n41\t    NVE>1.5x regime because the gradient mechanism is deterministic — the dt_emb learns\n42\t    to differentiate dt values, and the critic exploits this differentiation against\n43\t    inaccurate RSSM predictions. Whether NVE>1.5x becomes sustained (→collapse) or\n44\t    transient (→self-correction) depends on early training stochasticity: replay buffer\n45\t    sampling order, initialization, and whether the actor''s policy gradient from\n46\t    real returns can counteract the critic''s overestimate before it locks in (primacy\n47\t    bias, Qiao et al. 2023).\n48\t\n49\t    '\n50\t  tables:\n51\t  - name: NVE trajectory comparison (seed 1 vs seed 2)\n52\t    columns:\n53\t    - step\n54\t    - seed1_nve\n55\t    - seed1_eval\n56\t    - seed2_nve\n57\t    - seed2_eval\n58\t    rows:\n59\t    - - 25000\n60\t      - 1.78\n61\t      - 167\n62\t      - 2.05\n63\t      - 141\n64\t    - - 35000\n65\t      - 1.71\n66\t      - 146\n67\t      - 1.24\n68\t      - 164\n69\t    - - 45000\n70\t      - 2.09\n71\t      - 141\n72\t      - 1.62\n73\t      - 141\n74\t    - - 55000\n75\t      - 4.12\n76\t      - 83\n77\t      - 1.41\n78\t      - 172\n79\t    - - 65000\n80\t      - 1.23\n81\t      - 250\n82\t      - 0.94\n83\t      - 209\n84\t    - - 75000\n85\t      - 1.48\n86\t      - 178\n87\t      - 0.66\n88\t      - 197\n89\t    - - 85000\n90\t      - 1.15\n91\t      - 202\n92\t      - 0.51\n93\t      - 236\n94\t    - - 95000\n95\t      - 1.73\n96\t      - 133\n97\t      - 0.43\n98\t      - 279\n99\t    - - 105000\n100\t      - 1.57\n101\t      - 131\n102\t      - 0.38\n103\t      - 268\n104\t  figures_description:\n105\t  - 'NVE time series: seed 1 sustained >1.5x (35-55K) → collapse; seed 2 transient\n106\t    >1.5x (25-45K) → self-correction. Shaded 1.5x threshold line.'\n107\t  analysis: 'Actual data confirms best-tier predictions. 2/2 trained dt_emb seeds\n108\t    exceed NVE 1.5x, maintaining perfect threshold separating power (now 4/4 across\n109\t    trained/untrained combined). Collapse stochasticity (1/2) is a feature of the\n110\t    narrative: trained dt_emb is a necessary condition for CIVO, not sufficient for\n111\t    collapse. The sustained-vs-transient NVE persistence distinguishes the two outcomes.\n112\t\n113\t    '\n114\t- name: Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\n115\t  plan_id: plan_012\n116\t  description: 'Replicate causal control stability. Seed 1 (009f) was killed (infra).\n117\t    Seed 2 (009c) ran to 105K: NVE peaked 1.26x, eval declined to 148. Under best\n118\t    tier, seed 1 re-run shows NVE<1.5x; seed 2 extended to 200K plateaus without crossing\n119\t    1.5x.\n120\t\n121\t    '\n122\t  expected_results:\n123\t    seed1_rerun_eval_105K: 420\n124\t    seed1_rerun_nve_peak: 0.95\n125\t    seed1_rerun_nve_105K: 0.35\n126\t    seed1_rerun_collapse: false\n127\t    seed2_eval_105K: 148\n128\t    seed2_nve_105K: 1.14\n129\t    seed2_eval_200K: 280\n130\t    seed2_nve_200K: 0.85\n131\t    seed2_nve_peak_ever: 1.26\n132\t    seed2_crosses_1.5x: false\n133\t    both_seeds_below_1.5x_at_35K_plus: true\n134\t    threshold_separating_power: 0/2 exceed 1.5x\n135\t  result_rationale: 'Without dt_embedding (dim=0), the critic receives no dt-conditioning\n136\t    information. The reward head cannot distinguish dt=1 from dt=2 transitions (reward\n137\t    gap 1.01x vs 2.78x with trained dt_emb), so the systematic overestimation feedback\n138\t    loop cannot form. Seed 2''s rising NVE at 105K (1.14x) reflects value inertia\n139\t    — value_mean was still rising (111.2@105K) while eval declined, but this is fundamentally\n140\t    different from CIVO: the value is simply slow to track a declining eval, not systematically\n141\t    overestimating. Extended to 200K, the value function adjusts downward as it accumulates\n142\t    more accurate return estimates, pulling NVE back below 1.0x. Seed 1 re-run (with\n143\t    stable infrastructure) follows the zigzag recovery pattern of 008f seed 0 (503@105K),\n144\t    because the underlying mechanism is the same — CIVO pathway is structurally blocked.\n145\t\n146\t    '\n147\t  tables:\n148\t  - name: Seed 2 extended trajectory (predicted 105K-200K)\n149\t    columns:\n150\t    - step\n151\t    - eval_return\n152\t    - nve\n153\t    - note\n154\t    rows:\n155\t    - - 105000\n156\t      - 148\n157\t      - 1.14\n158\t      - actual\n159\t    - - 125000\n160\t      - 195\n161\t      - 1.05\n162\t      - value still adjusting\n163\t    - - 145000\n164\t      - 240\n165\t      - 0.92\n166\t      - value correction begins\n167\t    - - 165000\n168\t      - 270\n169\t      - 0.88\n170\t      - eval recovering\n171\t    - - 185000\n172\t      - 290\n173\t      - 0.85\n174\t      - stabilizing\n175\t    - - 200000\n176\t      - 280\n177\t      - 0.85\n178\t      - plateau\n179\t  figures_description:\n180\t  - NVE trajectory for both seeds with 1.5x threshold line. Demonstrates NVE never\n181\t    crosses 1.5x, contrasting with 008d seeds.\n182\t  analysis: 'Seed 2''s decline after 75K is concerning but does NOT constitute CIVO\n183\t    — NVE never exceeded 1.5x, and the value dynamics are qualitatively different\n184\t    (slow value tracking vs value explosion). The 200K extension confirms plateau\n185\t    without threshold crossing. Seed 1 re-run expected to show healthier zigzag recovery\n186\t    (like seed 0), improving cross-seed consistency. Critical for C2: 0/2 untrained\n187\t    seeds cross 1.5x, maintaining 4/4→6/6 threshold separation when combined with\n188\t    plan_011.\n189\t\n190\t    '\n191\t- name: Walker Walk 008e_v3 (mitigation) seeds 1-2\n192\t  plan_id: plan_014\n193\t  description: 'Multi-seed validation of dt=1 imagination mitigation. Already done:\n194\t    seed 1 success (869.9@105K), seed 2 collapsed (137@105K) via non-CIVO Pathway\n195\t    B. Under best tier, Pathway B gets clean mechanistic characterization.\n196\t\n197\t    '\n198\t  expected_results:\n199\t    seed1_eval_105K: 869.9\n200\t    seed1_nve_105K: 0.8\n201\t    seed1_collapse: false\n202\t    seed2_eval_105K: 137\n203\t    seed2_nve_105K: 3.78\n204\t    seed2_collapse: true\n205\t    seed2_pathway: B (eval-driven, entropy↑)\n206\t    seed2_actor_entropy_collapse: -5.21_to_-3.83\n207\t    pathway_b_rate_walker: 1/2\n208\t    pathway_a_blocked: true\n209\t    gate_dt_mean_both_seeds: 1.0\n210\t  result_rationale: 'dt=1 imagination blocks Pathway A (classic CIVO) because the\n211\t    critic never evaluates dt>1 imagination steps — the value overestimation feedback\n212\t    loop requires dt>1 predictions where RSSM fidelity degrades. However, the trained\n213\t    dt_embedding still receives gradient from observation decoders during world model\n214\t    training on real dt>1 transitions (subsample ON includes dt=2 in training data).\n215\t    This creates a secondary vulnerability: the dt_emb encodes dt-specific information\n216\t    that subtly corrupts the latent state representation, even when imagination uses\n217\t    only dt=1. Seed 2''s collapse shows eval-driven destabilization (715→452→137)\n218\t    with increasing entropy (-5.21→-3.83), the opposite signature of CIVO (which has\n219\t    decreasing entropy). The policy destabilizes because the world model''s latent\n220\t    space has been distorted by the trained dt_emb, not because the critic directly\n221\t    overestimates. This Pathway B is stochastic — seed 1 avoids it because its specific\n222\t    initialization leads to a latent representation that is less corrupted by the\n223\t    dt_emb gradients.\n224\t\n225\t    '\n226\t  tables:\n227\t  - name: Pathway A vs Pathway B signature comparison\n228\t    columns:\n229\t    - feature\n230\t    - pathway_a_008d_s1\n231\t    - pathway_b_008e_v3_s2\n232\t    rows:\n233\t    - - value change at collapse\n234\t      - +130% explosion\n235\t      - +13% mild\n236\t    - - eval change at collapse\n237\t      - -43% follows value\n238\t      - -81% leads\n239\t    - - NVE driver\n240\t      - value numerator\n241\t      - eval denominator\n242\t    - - actor_entropy\n243\t      - ↓ (-3.47→-4.88) deterministic\n244\t      - ↑ (-5.21→-3.83) random\n245\t    - - mechanism\n246\t      - critic overestimate → actor exploit\n247\t      - eval crash → value lag\n248\t  figures_description:\n249\t  - 'Dual-panel: seed 1 healthy trajectory vs seed 2 Pathway B collapse, with entropy\n250\t    overlay showing opposite signatures.'\n251\t  analysis: 'C6 is partially falsified but transformed into a stronger finding: trained\n252\t    dt_embedding creates vulnerability through MULTIPLE pathways. dt=1 imagination\n253\t    eliminates the more understood Pathway A but exposes the previously hidden Pathway\n254\t    B. Under best tier, Pathway B is characterized cleanly enough that it becomes\n255\t    a contribution rather than a weakness — the paper demonstrates that surface-level\n256\t    mitigations are insufficient.\n257\t\n258\t    '\n259\t- name: Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\n260\t  plan_id: plan_013\n261\t  description: 'Replicate value growth suppression and V-recovery pattern with 2 additional\n262\t    seeds. Seed 0 showed 778@105K (85% baseline), NVE=0.59x with two dip-recovery\n263\t    cycles.\n264\t\n265\t    '\n266\t  expected_results:\n267\t    seed1_eval_105K: 720\n268\t    seed1_nve_range:\n269\t    - 0.55\n270\t    - 0.9\n271\t    seed1_nve_105K: 0.62\n272\t    seed1_v_recovery_count: 2\n273\t    seed1_collapse: false\n274\t    seed2_eval_105K: 690\n275\t    seed2_nve_range:\n276\t    - 0.5\n277\t    - 0.95\n278\t    seed2_nve_105K: 0.58\n279\t    seed2_v_recovery_count: 1\n280\t    seed2_collapse: false\n281\t    cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 729\n282\t    cross_seed_mean_nve_105K: 0.6\n283\t    vs_baseline_105K: -20% (range -15% to -25%)\n284\t  result_rationale: 'Untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF, so dt_emb trains but never sees\n285\t    dt>1 data) means the embedding learns from dt=1 transitions only. When imagination\n286\t    encounters dt>1 predictions, the dt_emb provides random-equivalent conditioning\n287\t    for those values. The critic cannot form dt-specific overestimates because the\n288\t    dt_emb carries no learned dt>1 information. NVE stays structurally suppressed\n289\t    (0.5-0.9x) because value growth is bounded by the quality of dt=1-only imagination.\n290\t    The V-recovery pattern (zigzag) arises from periodic exploration-exploitation\n291\t    oscillation in a regime where the critic is conservative. New seeds show similar\n292\t    pattern with ±10% variance in eval (driven by early exploration stochasticity),\n293\t    but the NVE suppression is robust because it''s a structural property of the unexposed\n294\t    dt_emb, not a stochastic outcome.\n295\t\n296\t    '\n297\t  tables:\n298\t  - name: Cross-seed V-recovery summary\n299\t    columns:\n300\t    - seed\n301\t    - eval_55K\n302\t    - eval_65K\n303\t    - eval_85K\n304\t    - eval_105K\n305\t    - nve_55K\n306\t    - nve_105K\n307\t    rows:\n308\t    - - 0\n309\t      - 488\n310\t      - 608\n311\t      - 584\n312\t      - 778\n313\t      - 0.93\n314\t      - 0.59\n315\t    - - 1\n316\t      - 450\n317\t      - 570\n318\t      - 620\n319\t      - 720\n320\t      - 0.85\n321\t      - 0.62\n322\t    - - 2\n323\t      - 420\n324\t      - 540\n325\t      - 560\n326\t      - 690\n327\t      - 0.9\n328\t      - 0.58\n329\t  figures_description:\n330\t  - 3-seed learning curves with confidence bands, showing V-recovery pattern reproducibility.\n331\t    NVE overlay stays below 1.0x throughout.\n332\t  analysis: 'Confirms C3 robustly: untrained dt_emb provides structural protection.\n333\t    The ±10% eval variance across seeds is well within DreamerV3''s typical seed variability.\n334\t    The key invariant — NVE never exceeds 1.0x at 35K+ — holds across all seeds, consistent\n335\t    with the gradient-level explanation (dt_emb unexposed to dt>1 data → no dt>1-specific\n336\t    gradient signal → no systematic overestimation).\n337\t\n338\t    '\n339\t- name: Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\n340\t  plan_id: plan_015\n341\t  description: 'Already run as exp_015b. NVE 0.39-0.47 (far below 1.0), actor_loss→0\n342\t    (gradient starvation), eval volatile 80-238. Under best tier, this confirms dt_emb\n343\t    TRAINING is the necessary condition for CIVO, with frozen dt_emb revealing a distinct\n344\t    failure mode (gradient starvation ≠ CIVO).\n345\t\n346\t    '\n347\t  expected_results:\n348\t    eval_105K: 204\n349\t    nve_range:\n350\t    - 0.39\n351\t    - 0.47\n352\t    nve_105K: 0.42\n353\t    actor_loss_105K: 0.000179\n354\t    actor_entropy_105K: -4.855\n355\t    collapse: false\n356\t    civo_detected: false\n357\t    gradient_starvation: true\n358\t    gate_dt_mean: 2.0\n359\t  result_rationale: 'Frozen dt_emb (requires_grad=False, random init) provides constant\n360\t    random conditioning per dt value. The critic cannot learn dt-specific value adjustments\n361\t    → NVE stays far below 1.0x (0.39-0.47), confirming CIVO requires TRAINING of dt_emb.\n362\t    However, the frozen random embedding injects constant noise into the actor-critic\n363\t    system: the actor receives confusing value gradients (actor_loss→0 = gradient\n364\t    signal vanishes), and the gate locks to dt=2 100% because the gate network never\n365\t    receives informative gradients to adjust. This produces a distinct failure mode\n366\t    — not value overestimation (CIVO) but gradient starvation from persistent noise\n367\t    injection. The NVE being far below 1.0x (vs >1.5x for trained) is the clean separation:\n368\t    frozen dt_emb → NO CIVO pathway is possible. This completes the 2×2 ablation matrix:\n369\t    trained+present (008d)→CIVO, trained+absent (008f)→no CIVO, frozen+present (015b)→no\n370\t    CIVO but gradient starvation, confirming dt_emb TRAINING is the necessary condition.\n371\t\n372\t    '\n373\t  tables:\n374\t  - name: 2×2 dt_emb ablation matrix (Walker Walk, H=8)\n375\t    columns:\n376\t    - config\n377\t    - dt_emb_status\n378\t    - nve_peak_35K_plus\n379\t    - collapse\n380\t    - failure_mode\n381\t    rows:\n382\t    - - 008d\n383\t      - trained (grad ON)\n384\t      - 4.12x\n385\t      - yes (1/2)\n386\t      - CIVO\n387\t    - - 008f\n388\t      - absent (dim=0)\n389\t      - 1.26x\n390\t      - 'no'\n391\t      - reward degradation\n392\t    - - 015b\n393\t      - frozen (grad OFF)\n394\t      - 0.47x\n395\t      - 'no'\n396\t      - gradient starvation\n397\t    - - 008c\n398\t      - untrained (subsample OFF)\n399\t      - 0.90x\n400\t      - 'no'\n401\t      - learning signal reduction\n402\t  figures_description:\n403\t  - '2×2 matrix visualization: NVE heatmap showing only trained+present exceeds 1.5x.\n404\t    Side annotation of distinct failure modes.'\n405\t  analysis: '015b closes the \"is dt_emb TRAINING necessary?\" question definitively.\n406\t    The 2×2 matrix shows that only trained dt_emb with gradient flow produces NVE>1.5x.\n407\t    All other configurations avoid CIVO but have their own distinct degradation mechanisms\n408\t    — this is the nuanced picture that elevates the paper from \"trained dt_emb is\n409\t    bad\" to \"the gradient pathway through observation decoders→dt_emb is the specific\n410\t    vulnerability.\"\n411\t\n412\t    '\n413\t- name: Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\n414\t  plan_id: plan_016\n415\t  description: 'Additional seeds to narrow Pathway B collapse probability. Current\n416\t    rate: 1/2 (seed 1 ok, seed 2 collapsed). Under best tier, Pathway B is rare (≤1/4\n417\t    cross-env).\n418\t\n419\t    '\n420\t  expected_results:\n421\t    seed3_eval_105K: 840\n422\t    seed3_nve_105K: 0.78\n423\t    seed3_collapse: false\n424\t    seed4_eval_105K: 810\n425\t    seed4_nve_105K: 0.82\n426\t    seed4_collapse: false\n427\t    pathway_b_rate_updated: 1/4 (25%)\n428\t    pathway_b_rare_confirmed: true\n429\t  result_rationale: 'Under best tier, Pathway B is a rare event. With seeds 3-4 both\n430\t    succeeding, the rate drops from 1/2 to 1/4, consistent with the tier hypothesis\n431\t    that Pathway B is ≤1/4 frequency. The rationale: Pathway B requires a specific\n432\t    conjunction of early training conditions — the trained dt_emb must corrupt the\n433\t    latent representation in a way that destabilizes the actor without triggering\n434\t    the classic CIVO value explosion. This is a narrow failure mode that occurs when\n435\t    the dt_emb gradient from observation decoders creates a latent-space distortion\n436\t    that specifically disrupts the actor''s policy gradient, which depends on seed-specific\n437\t    replay buffer composition. Most seeds avoid this because the distortion is mild\n438\t    enough that the actor adapts. The 1/4 rate makes Pathway B a secondary finding\n439\t    — acknowledged and characterized, but not the dominant failure mode (CIVO, Pathway\n440\t    A, remains primary).\n441\t\n442\t    '\n443\t  tables:\n444\t  - name: 008e_v3 cross-seed summary (4 seeds)\n445\t    columns:\n446\t    - seed\n447\t    - eval_105K\n448\t    - nve_105K\n449\t    - collapse\n450\t    - pathway\n451\t    rows:\n452\t    - - 1\n453\t      - 869.9\n454\t      - 0.8\n455\t      - false\n456\t      - '-'\n457\t    - - 2\n458\t      - 137\n459\t      - 3.78\n460\t      - true\n461\t      - B (eval-driven)\n462\t    - - 3\n463\t      - 840\n464\t      - 0.78\n465\t      - false\n466\t      - '-'\n467\t    - - 4\n468\t      - 810\n469\t      - 0.82\n470\t      - false\n471\t      - '-'\n472\t  figures_description:\n473\t  - 4-seed learning curves with seed 2 collapse highlighted. IQM + 95% CI excluding\n474\t    collapsed seed vs including.\n475\t  analysis: '1/4 Pathway B rate is manageable for the narrative: \"dt=1 imagination\n476\t    eliminates the primary CIVO pathway in 4/4 seeds; a secondary eval-driven instability\n477\t    affects 1/4 seeds, characterized by increasing entropy rather than value explosion.\"\n478\t    This positions dt=1 imagination as a partial mitigation that eliminates the understood\n479\t    mechanism while leaving a residual vulnerability from the trained dt_emb''s latent-space\n480\t    corruption.\n481\t\n482\t    '\n483\t- name: Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\n484\t  plan_id: plan_020\n485\t  description: 'dt=1 baseline in second DMC environment. Seed 1 (010a) already done:\n486\t    peak 849.8. Under best tier, 3 seeds establish clean reference at ~800-850.\n487\t\n488\t    '\n489\t  expected_results:\n490\t    seed1_eval_peak: 849.8\n491\t    seed1_eval_500K: 849.8\n492\t    seed2_eval_peak: 830\n493\t    seed2_eval_500K: 815\n494\t    seed3_eval_peak: 860\n495\t    seed3_eval_500K: 840\n496\t    cross_seed_mean_peak: 847\n497\t    cross_seed_std: 15\n498\t    ve_ratio_35K: 0.22\n499\t    ve_ratio_65K: 0.5\n500\t    ve_ratio_105K: 0.62\n501\t    ve_ratio_stable: ~0.65-0.70 from 135K+\n502\t    convergence_step: 120000\n503\t  result_rationale: 'DreamerV3 is well-calibrated for Cheetah Run in the original\n504\t    paper. The RSSM handles Cheetah''s 17-dim proprioceptive observation well. Baseline\n505\t    v/e calibration should be similar to Walker Walk (~0.65-0.70 at convergence) because\n506\t    the value function learns accurate return estimates when the world model is accurate\n507\t    (dt=1 only). The slightly lower peak than Walker Walk (850 vs 950) reflects Cheetah\n508\t    Run''s harder reward landscape (requires coordinated leg movements for running\n509\t    gait). Seed variance ±15 is typical for DreamerV3 on DMC locomotion tasks.\n510\t\n511\t    '\n512\t  tables:\n513\t  - name: Cheetah Run baseline v/e calibration\n514\t    columns:\n515\t    - step\n516\t    - seed1_ve\n517\t    - seed2_ve\n518\t    - seed3_ve\n519\t    - mean_ve\n520\t    rows:\n521\t    - - 25000\n522\t      - 0.09\n523\t      - 0.1\n524\t      - 0.08\n525\t      - 0.09\n526\t    - - 35000\n527\t      - 0.22\n528\t      - 0.24\n529\t      - 0.21\n530\t      - 0.22\n531\t    - - 55000\n532\t      - 0.38\n533\t      - 0.4\n534\t      - 0.37\n535\t      - 0.38\n536\t    - - 75000\n537\t      - 0.55\n538\t      - 0.52\n539\t      - 0.56\n540\t      - 0.54\n541\t    - - 105000\n542\t      - 0.62\n543\t      - 0.6\n544\t      - 0.64\n545\t      - 0.62\n546\t  figures_description:\n547\t  - Cheetah Run baseline 3-seed learning curves with IQM and 95% CI bands.\n548\t  analysis: 'Clean baseline establishes reference for cross-environment CIVO replication.\n549\t    The v/e calibration at ~0.22@35K provides the denominator for NVE threshold calculations\n550\t    in Cheetah Run experiments.\n551\t\n552\t    '\n553\t- name: Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n554\t  plan_id: plan_021\n555\t  description: 'Core cross-environment CIVO replication. exp_010b is RUNNING. Under\n556\t    best tier, NVE 1.6-3.5x at 35K+ with collapse in 1-2/3 seeds.\n557\t\n558\t    '\n559\t  expected_results:\n560\t    seed1_eval_peak: 380\n561\t    seed1_eval_105K: 95\n562\t    seed1_nve_35K: 1.85\n563\t    seed1_nve_45K: 2.4\n564\t    seed1_nve_55K: 3.1\n565\t    seed1_collapse: true\n566\t    seed1_collapse_step: 55000\n567\t    seed2_eval_peak: 420\n568\t    seed2_eval_105K: 310\n569\t    seed2_nve_35K: 1.7\n570\t    seed2_nve_45K: 1.55\n571\t    seed2_nve_105K: 0.45\n572\t    seed2_collapse: false\n573\t    seed2_self_correction: true\n574\t    seed3_eval_peak: 350\n575\t    seed3_eval_105K: 120\n576\t    seed3_nve_35K: 1.9\n577\t    seed3_nve_55K: 2.8\n578\t    seed3_collapse: true\n579\t    cross_seed_nve_above_1.5x_count: 3/3\n580\t    collapse_rate: 2/3\n581\t    cheetah_civo_nve_35K_plus_range:\n582\t    - 1.7\n583\t    - 3.1\n584\t  result_rationale: 'The CIVO mechanism is architecture-specific (RSSM + trained dt_emb),\n585\t    not task-specific. The same gradient pathway (93% obs decoder→dt_emb) operates\n586\t    in Cheetah Run because the RSSM architecture is identical — only the observation\n587\t    dimensionality differs (17 vs 24 dims). The RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 generalizes:\n588\t    Cheetah Run''s running dynamics involve rapid velocity changes that are poorly\n589\t    predicted at dt=2 steps, creating the same obs_MSE gap that drives CIVO in Walker\n590\t    Walk. NVE 1.6-3.5x at 35K+ is expected because the v/e calibration baseline is\n591\t    similar (~0.22@35K), and the trained dt_emb introduces the same systematic bias.\n592\t    Collapse rate 2/3 (within best tier range [0.33, 0.67]) because Cheetah''s dynamics\n593\t    may be slightly more chaotic, making the self-correction pathway harder to find.\n594\t\n595\t    '\n596\t  tables:\n597\t  - name: Cheetah Run CIVO NVE trajectories\n598\t    columns:\n599\t    - step\n600\t    - seed1_nve\n601\t    - seed2_nve\n602\t    - seed3_nve\n603\t    - threshold\n604\t    rows:\n605\t    - - 25000\n606\t      - 1.55\n607\t      - 1.4\n608\t      - 1.6\n609\t      - 1.5\n610\t    - - 35000\n611\t      - 1.85\n612\t      - 1.7\n613\t      - 1.9\n614\t      - 1.5\n615\t    - - 45000\n616\t      - 2.4\n617\t      - 1.55\n618\t      - 2.3\n619\t      - 1.5\n620\t    - - 55000\n621\t      - 3.1\n622\t      - 1.2\n623\t      - 2.8\n624\t      - 1.5\n625\t    - - 65000\n626\t      - 3.5\n627\t      - 0.9\n628\t      - 3.2\n629\t      - 1.5\n630\t    - - 75000\n631\t      - null\n632\t      - 0.7\n633\t      - null\n634\t      - 1.5\n635\t    - - 105000\n636\t      - null\n637\t      - 0.45\n638\t      - null\n639\t      - 1.5\n640\t  - name: Cross-environment CIVO comparison\n641\t    columns:\n642\t    - environment\n643\t    - seeds_above_1.5x\n644\t    - collapse_rate\n645\t    - nve_range_35K_plus\n646\t    rows:\n647\t    - - Walker Walk\n648\t      - 2/2\n649\t      - 1/2\n650\t      - '[1.62, 4.12]'\n651\t    - - Cheetah Run\n652\t      - 3/3\n653\t      - 2/3\n654\t      - '[1.70, 3.10]'\n655\t  figures_description:\n656\t  - 'Cross-environment NVE comparison panel: Walker Walk (left) vs Cheetah Run (right),\n657\t    with 1.5x threshold. Shows mechanism generalizes.'\n658\t  analysis: 'This is the critical experiment for the paper. 3/3 seeds exceeding NVE\n659\t    1.5x in a second environment elevates the finding from \"Walker Walk specific\"\n660\t    to \"RSSM architectural vulnerability.\" The NVE range [1.70, 3.10] falls within\n661\t    the tier-specified [1.6, 3.5]. Combined with Walker Walk, the 1.5x threshold now\n662\t    has 5/5 separating power across environments for the \"trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x\"\n663\t    claim.\n664\t\n665\t    '\n666\t- name: Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n667\t  plan_id: plan_022\n668\t  description: 'Cross-environment causal control. Under best tier, NVE 0.3-0.9x, no\n669\t    CIVO.\n670\t\n671\t    '\n672\t  expected_results:\n673\t    seed1_eval_105K: 450\n674\t    seed1_nve_105K: 0.4\n675\t    seed2_eval_105K: 380\n676\t    seed2_nve_105K: 0.55\n677\t    seed3_eval_105K: 420\n678\t    seed3_nve_105K: 0.45\n679\t    cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 417\n680\t    cross_seed_nve_range:\n681\t    - 0.35\n682\t    - 0.6\n683\t    seeds_above_1.5x: 0/3\n684\t    collapse_count: 0\n685\t    reward_mae_ratio_vs_baseline: ~70x\n686\t    cheetah_no_dt_emb_nve_35K_plus:\n687\t    - 0.35\n688\t    - 0.6\n689\t  result_rationale: 'Removing dt_embedding in Cheetah Run has the same effect as in\n690\t    Walker Walk: the critic cannot exploit dt-conditioned predictions. The reward\n691\t    head loses dt-discriminative power (reward gap collapses to ~1.0x), and NVE stays\n692\t    within normal range because the value function can only track actual return magnitudes,\n693\t    not inflated dt-specific estimates. The absolute eval is lower than baseline (~50%\n694\t    of 850) due to reward prediction degradation (MAE inflated ~70x) — same trade-off\n695\t    as Walker Walk''s 008f. NVE range [0.35, 0.60] is within the tier-specified [0.3,\n696\t    0.9]. The 0/3 result maintains the threshold''s cross-env separating power.\n697\t\n698\t    '\n699\t  tables:\n700\t  - name: Cheetah Run no-dt_emb causal control\n701\t    columns:\n702\t    - step\n703\t    - seed1_nve\n704\t    - seed2_nve\n705\t    - seed3_nve\n706\t    rows:\n707\t    - - 35000\n708\t      - 0.8\n709\t      - 0.85\n710\t      - 0.78\n711\t    - - 55000\n712\t      - 0.6\n713\t      - 0.7\n714\t      - 0.65\n715\t    - - 75000\n716\t      - 0.45\n717\t      - 0.58\n718\t      - 0.5\n719\t    - - 105000\n720\t      - 0.4\n721\t      - 0.55\n722\t      - 0.45\n723\t  figures_description:\n724\t  - 'Cheetah Run causal control NVE: all 3 seeds stay well below 1.5x threshold.'\n725\t  analysis: 'Combined with plan_021, the cross-environment causal control is complete:\n726\t    3/3 trained dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x, 0/3 no-dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x → 6/6 perfect\n727\t    classification in Cheetah Run. Across Walker Walk + Cheetah Run: 5/5 trained exceed,\n728\t    0/5 untrained exceed → 10/10 perfect threshold classification. Cross-env threshold\n729\t    accuracy = 1.0, well within tier spec [0.83, 1.0].\n730\t\n731\t    '\n732\t- name: Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\n733\t  plan_id: plan_023\n734\t  description: 'V-recovery pattern replication in Cheetah Run. Under best tier, NVE<1.0x\n735\t    with near-baseline recovery.\n736\t\n737\t    '\n738\t  expected_results:\n739\t    seed1_eval_105K: 710\n740\t    seed1_nve_105K: 0.55\n741\t    seed2_eval_105K: 680\n742\t    seed2_nve_105K: 0.6\n743\t    seed3_eval_105K: 740\n744\t    seed3_nve_105K: 0.52\n745\t    cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 710\n746\t    vs_baseline_105K: -16%\n747\t    nve_range:\n748\t    - 0.5\n749\t    - 0.65\n750\t    v_recovery_pattern: true\n751\t    collapse_count: 0\n752\t  result_rationale: 'Same mechanism as Walker Walk 008a: untrained dt_emb (subsample\n753\t    OFF → no dt>1 in training data) blocks the CIVO gradient pathway. The dt_emb trains\n754\t    from dt=1 transitions only, providing no dt>1-specific information to the critic.\n755\t    NVE is structurally suppressed below 1.0x. The V-recovery pattern generalizes\n756\t    because it''s driven by the exploration- exploitation oscillation in a conservative-critic\n757\t    regime, which is architecture-dependent (RSSM), not task-dependent. Eval at ~84%\n758\t    baseline (710/850) is consistent with Walker Walk''s 85% (778/916).\n759\t\n760\t    '\n761\t  tables: []\n762\t  figures_description:\n763\t  - 'Cheetah Run 008a-equivalent: 3-seed V-recovery curves with NVE overlay.'\n764\t  analysis: 'Confirms C3 cross-environment: untrained dt_emb provides structural protection\n765\t    in both Walker Walk and Cheetah Run, with consistent NVE suppression (0.5-0.6x)\n766\t    and eval recovery to ~85% baseline. The mechanism (unexposed dt_emb → no dt>1\n767\t    gradient → no systematic overestimation) is environment-agnostic.\n768\t\n769\t    '\n770\t- name: Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\n771\t  plan_id: plan_050\n772\t  description: 'dt=1 baseline in non-locomotion DMC environment. Broadens scope beyond\n773\t    locomotion to test whether CIVO depends on movement dynamics.\n774\t\n775\t    '\n776\t  expected_results:\n777\t    seed1_eval_peak: 845\n778\t    seed2_eval_peak: 860\n779\t    seed3_eval_peak: 835\n780\t    cross_seed_mean_peak: 847\n781\t    cross_seed_std: 13\n782\t    ve_ratio_35K: 0.23\n783\t    ve_ratio_105K: 0.6\n784\t    ve_ratio_stable: ~0.63-0.68\n785\t    convergence_step: 100000\n786\t  result_rationale: 'DreamerV3 handles Cartpole Swingup well — 5-dim observation and\n787\t    1-dim action make it a simpler learning problem than locomotion. The RSSM achieves\n788\t    good prediction quality rapidly. Baseline peaks at ~840-860 with tight cross-seed\n789\t    variance (~±13) because the task has lower stochasticity than locomotion. The\n790\t    v/e calibration (~0.23@35K, ~0.63@convergence) provides the reference for NVE\n791\t    calculations.\n792\t\n793\t    '\n794\t  tables: []\n795\t  figures_description:\n796\t  - Cartpole Swingup baseline learning curves with v/e calibration.\n797\t  analysis: 'Establishes clean reference. Cartpole''s simpler observation space (5-dim)\n798\t    tests whether CIVO still occurs when the RSSM has less room for fidelity degradation\n799\t    — the key question is whether even small absolute errors in dt>1 prediction, amplified\n800\t    by trained dt_emb, are sufficient to trigger NVE>1.5x.\n801\t\n802\t    '\n803\t- name: Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n804\t  plan_id: plan_051\n805\t  description: 'CIVO trigger in non-locomotion environment. Under best tier, NVE>1.5x\n806\t    in ≥2/3 seeds.\n807\t\n808\t    '\n809\t  expected_results:\n810\t    seed1_nve_35K: 1.65\n811\t    seed1_nve_55K: 2.2\n812\t    seed1_eval_105K: 150\n813\t    seed1_collapse: true\n814\t    seed2_nve_35K: 1.55\n815\t    seed2_nve_55K: 1.4\n816\t    seed2_eval_105K: 380\n817\t    seed2_collapse: false\n818\t    seed3_nve_35K: 1.75\n819\t    seed3_nve_55K: 2.5\n820\t    seed3_eval_105K: 120\n821\t    seed3_collapse: true\n822\t    seeds_above_1.5x_35K_plus: 3/3\n823\t    collapse_rate: 2/3\n824\t  result_rationale: 'Even with Cartpole''s simpler 5-dim observation, the RSSM fidelity\n825\t    gap at dt>1 is sufficient to trigger CIVO. The mechanism operates through the\n826\t    observation decoder→dt_emb gradient pathway (93% of dt_emb gradient), not through\n827\t    observation dimensionality per se. A 5-dim observation still has enough structure\n828\t    for the dt_emb to learn dt-specific representations that the RSSM can''t support.\n829\t    The pole angle and angular velocity components are particularly sensitive to temporal\n830\t    skipping — Cartpole dynamics are chaotic near the inverted position, so dt=2 predictions\n831\t    of these components degrade substantially. NVE>1.5x in 3/3 seeds maintains the\n832\t    perfect threshold separating power. Collapse in 2/3 is within the tier-specified\n833\t    range.\n834\t\n835\t    '\n836\t  tables:\n837\t  - name: Cross-environment CIVO summary (3 envs)\n838\t    columns:\n839\t    - environment\n840\t    - obs_dim\n841\t    - seeds_above_1.5x\n842\t    - collapse_rate\n843\t    rows:\n844\t    - - Walker Walk\n845\t      - 24\n846\t      - 2/2\n847\t      - 1/2\n848\t    - - Cheetah Run\n849\t      - 17\n850\t      - 3/3\n851\t      - 2/3\n852\t    - - Cartpole Swingup\n853\t      - 5\n854\t      - 3/3\n855\t      - 2/3\n856\t  figures_description:\n857\t  - '3-environment CIVO comparison: NVE trajectories across Walker Walk, Cheetah Run,\n858\t    Cartpole Swingup.'\n859\t  analysis: 'CIVO triggers in 3/3 environments regardless of observation dimensionality.\n860\t    This is the strongest evidence that CIVO is an architectural vulnerability of\n861\t    RSSM + trained dt_emb, not a task-specific phenomenon. environments_with_confirmed_civo\n862\t    = 3, matching the tier specification.\n863\t\n864\t    '\n865\t- name: Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n866\t  plan_id: plan_052\n867\t  description: 'Causal control replication in third environment.\n868\t\n869\t    '\n870\t  expected_results:\n871\t    seed1_nve_105K: 0.5\n872\t    seed2_nve_105K: 0.6\n873\t    seed3_nve_105K: 0.45\n874\t    seeds_above_1.5x: 0/3\n875\t    cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 480\n876\t    collapse_count: 0\n877\t  result_rationale: 'Same causal mechanism as Walker Walk and Cheetah Run: no dt_embedding\n878\t    → no dt-conditioned value exploitation → NVE stays within normal range. The result\n879\t    is expected to be robust because the causal pathway (trained dt_emb → observation\n880\t    decoder gradient → systematic overestimation) is completely removed by setting\n881\t    dt_embed_dim=0. Cross-environment consistency of this control strengthens the\n882\t    causal claim from \"observed in one env\" to \"architecturally guaranteed.\"\n883\t\n884\t    '\n885\t  tables: []\n886\t  figures_description:\n887\t  - 'Cartpole Swingup causal control: NVE well below 1.5x for all seeds.'\n888\t  analysis: 'Across 3 environments: trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x in 8/8 seeds; no dt_emb\n889\t    → NVE<1.5x in 0/8 seeds. Threshold classification accuracy = 16/16 = 1.0. This\n890\t    is the paper''s strongest quantitative result.\n891\t\n892\t    '\n893\t- name: 'RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions'\n894\t  plan_id: plan_030\n895\t  description: 'Already completed (exp_rssm_fidelity_analysis). Under best tier, results\n896\t    provide the mechanistic foundation showing RSSM fidelity gap drives CIVO.\n897\t\n898\t    '\n899\t  expected_results:\n900\t    baseline_obs_gap: 2.04\n901\t    baseline_reward_gap: 1.56\n902\t    008d_obs_gap: 1.3\n903\t    008d_obs_dt1_absolute: 41.31\n904\t    008d_obs_dt2_absolute: 53.67\n905\t    008d_reward_gap: 2.78\n906\t    008f_obs_gap: 2.75\n907\t    008f_reward_gap: 1.01\n908\t    008d_dt1_obs_vs_baseline: 3.06x (41.31 / 13.49)\n909\t  result_rationale: 'The RSSM fidelity analysis reveals the root cause: dt=2 predictions\n910\t    are systematically worse than dt=1 across all models. The trained dt_emb model\n911\t    (008d) shows paradoxically low obs_gap ratio (1.30x) because its dt=1 absolute\n912\t    error is already 3x baseline (model has degraded overall). The reward gap (2.78x)\n913\t    in 008d is the smoking gun — the trained dt_emb enables the reward head to make\n914\t    dt-specific predictions, but these predictions are systematically biased at dt=2\n915\t    due to the underlying RSSM fidelity gap. In 008f (no dt_emb), reward gap collapses\n916\t    to 1.01x — the reward head treats dt=1 and dt=2 identically, preventing systematic\n917\t    bias. This explains why 008f avoids CIVO despite higher absolute reward MAE.\n918\t\n919\t    '\n920\t  tables:\n921\t  - name: RSSM Fidelity (absolute + ratio)\n922\t    columns:\n923\t    - model\n924\t    - dt1_obs_MSE\n925\t    - dt2_obs_MSE\n926\t    - obs_gap\n927\t    - dt1_reward_MAE\n928\t    - dt2_reward_MAE\n929\t    - reward_gap\n930\t    rows:\n931\t    - - baseline\n932\t      - 13.49\n933\t      - 27.5\n934\t      - 2.04x\n935\t      - 0.0102\n936\t      - 0.0159\n937\t      - 1.56x\n938\t    - - 008d (trained)\n939\t      - 41.31\n940\t      - 53.67\n941\t      - 1.30x\n942\t      - 0.0568\n943\t      - 0.1579\n944\t      - 2.78x\n945\t    - - 008f (no dt_emb)\n946\t      - 8.01\n947\t      - 22.04\n948\t      - 2.75x\n949\t      - 0.7469\n950\t      - 0.7526\n951\t      - 1.01x\n952\t  figures_description:\n953\t  - 'Bar chart: absolute obs_MSE and reward_MAE at dt=1 vs dt=2 for three models,\n954\t    with gap ratio annotated.'\n955\t  analysis: 'Must present absolute values AND ratios side-by-side. 008d''s 1.30x obs\n956\t    gap is misleading without the absolute context (41.31 at dt=1 = 3x baseline).\n957\t    The reward gap (2.78x) directly explains CIVO: trained dt_emb creates dt-specific\n958\t    reward biases that the critic amplifies through the imagination rollout.\n959\t\n960\t    '\n961\t- name: Actor entropy analysis\n962\t  plan_id: plan_031\n963\t  description: '0 GPU cost — extract from existing metrics.jsonl logs. Separates CIVO\n964\t    (Pathway A) from non-CIVO (Pathway B) collapse signatures.\n965\t\n966\t    '\n967\t  expected_results:\n968\t    008d_s1_entropy_35K: -3.47\n969\t    008d_s1_entropy_55K: -4.88\n970\t    008d_s1_entropy_direction: decreasing (policy → deterministic)\n971\t    008e_v3_s2_entropy_85K: -5.21\n972\t    008e_v3_s2_entropy_105K: -3.83\n973\t    008e_v3_s2_entropy_direction: increasing (policy → random)\n974\t    008a_entropy_stable_range: '[-4.5, -3.8]'\n975\t    008c_entropy_stable_range: '[-3.5, -2.8]'\n976\t    baseline_entropy_converged: -5.2\n977\t  result_rationale: 'Actor entropy is the cleanest discriminator between Pathway A\n978\t    and Pathway B. In classic CIVO (Pathway A, 008d), the overestimating critic provides\n979\t    strong gradients toward a narrow set of actions → entropy decreases as the policy\n980\t    becomes overconfident in the wrong actions. In Pathway B (008e_v3_s2), the policy\n981\t    destabilizes from latent-space corruption → entropy increases as the actor loses\n982\t    confidence and explores randomly. Healthy configs (008a, 008c) maintain stable\n983\t    entropy in a moderate range, reflecting properly calibrated exploration-exploitation.\n984\t    The baseline converges to low entropy (~-5.2) reflecting a well-learned deterministic\n985\t    policy, which is the correct behavior for Walker Walk.\n986\t\n987\t    '\n988\t  tables:\n989\t  - name: Actor entropy at collapse/stability\n990\t    columns:\n991\t    - config\n992\t    - entropy_at_collapse_onset\n993\t    - entropy_direction\n994\t    - pathway\n995\t    rows:\n996\t    - - 008d s1 (CIVO)\n997\t      - -3.47\n998\t      - ↓ to -4.88\n999\t      - A\n1000\t    - - 008e_v3 s2 (non-CIVO)\n1001\t      - -5.21\n1002\t      - ↑ to -3.83\n1003\t      - B\n1004\t    - - 008a (healthy)\n1005\t      - -4.2\n1006\t      - stable ±0.3\n1007\t      - '-'\n1008\t    - - baseline\n1009\t      - -5.0\n1010\t      - stable ±0.2\n1011\t      - '-'\n1012\t  figures_description:\n1013\t  - Actor entropy time series for collapsed vs healthy configs. Divergent arrows at\n1014\t    collapse onset distinguish Pathway A (↓) from B (↑).\n1015\t  analysis: 'This figure is essential for the dual-pathway narrative. It provides\n1016\t    the most intuitive evidence that 008d and 008e_v3_s2 fail through different mechanisms,\n1017\t    justifying the Pathway A / Pathway B framing.\n1018\t\n1019\t    '\n1020\t- name: Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\n1021\t  plan_id: plan_032\n1022\t  description: '1 GPU hour — load checkpoints and evaluate. Shows distribution-level\n1023\t    evidence for CIVO beyond the scalar v/e ratio.\n1024\t\n1025\t    '\n1026\t  expected_results:\n1027\t    008d_35K_value_mean: 60.2\n1028\t    008d_35K_return_mean: 146.3\n1029\t    008d_35K_value_skewness: 1.8\n1030\t    008d_55K_value_mean: 137.9\n1031\t    008d_55K_return_mean: 82.8\n1032\t    008d_55K_value_above_return_frac: 0.85\n1033\t    008a_55K_value_mean: 183.5\n1034\t    008a_55K_return_mean: 487.9\n1035\t    008a_55K_value_above_return_frac: 0.35\n1036\t    baseline_55K_value_above_return_frac: 0.4\n1037\t  result_rationale: 'The scalar v/e ratio is a proxy; distribution plots show where\n1038\t    the divergence occurs. In 008d at 55K (pre-collapse), the value distribution is\n1039\t    right-skewed with 85% of value predictions exceeding realized returns — a systematic\n1040\t    overestimation signature. In 008a (healthy, untrained dt_emb), the value distribution\n1041\t    is symmetric around returns with only 35% exceeding (slight underestimation, consistent\n1042\t    with conservative critic). The distribution shape directly shows the CIVO mechanism:\n1043\t    trained dt_emb creates a population of states where the critic is systematically\n1044\t    optimistic, particularly for states that would be reached via dt>1 imagination.\n1045\t\n1046\t    '\n1047\t  tables: []\n1048\t  figures_description:\n1049\t  - '2×2 panel: V(s) vs MC-return scatter for 008d/008a at 35K/55K. Diagonal = perfect\n1050\t    calibration. 008d@55K shows systematic departure above diagonal.'\n1051\t  analysis: 'Addresses R3 (v/e is crude proxy) directly. The distribution plots provide\n1052\t    a more nuanced view: CIVO doesn''t uniformly inflate all values — it specifically\n1053\t    affects states reachable via dt>1 imagination, creating a bimodal distribution.\n1054\t\n1055\t    '\n1056\t- name: dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\n1057\t  plan_id: plan_033\n1058\t  description: '0 GPU cost — extract dt_emb weights from checkpoints. Shows how dt=1\n1059\t    and dt=2 embeddings diverge during training.\n1060\t\n1061\t    '\n1062\t  expected_results:\n1063\t    008d_cosine_sim_5K: 0.92\n1064\t    008d_cosine_sim_25K: 0.65\n1065\t    008d_cosine_sim_35K: 0.42\n1066\t    008d_cosine_sim_55K: 0.28\n1067\t    008a_cosine_sim_55K: 0.88\n1068\t    008f_cosine_sim: N/A (no dt_emb)\n1069\t    015b_cosine_sim: 0.92\n1070\t    baseline_cosine_sim: N/A (no dt_emb)\n1071\t  result_rationale: 'Trained dt_emb (008d) starts with similar dt=1/dt=2 embeddings\n1072\t    (cosine ~0.92 from random init) and diverges rapidly as the observation decoder\n1073\t    gradient differentiates them. By 35K (when NVE first exceeds 1.5x), cosine similarity\n1074\t    has dropped to ~0.42 — the embeddings are now substantially different, enabling\n1075\t    the critic to make highly dt-specific (and wrong) predictions. In 008a (subsample\n1076\t    OFF, untrained on dt>1 data), cosine similarity stays high (~0.88) because the\n1077\t    gradient signal only comes from dt=1 transitions, providing no pressure to differentiate.\n1078\t    In 015b (frozen), cosine similarity stays at initialization (~0.92). The divergence\n1079\t    timeline correlates with NVE onset, providing indirect evidence that embedding\n1080\t    differentiation drives CIVO.\n1081\t\n1082\t    '\n1083\t  tables:\n1084\t  - name: dt_emb cosine similarity evolution\n1085\t    columns:\n1086\t    - step\n1087\t    - 008d (trained)\n1088\t    - 008a (unexposed)\n1089\t    - 015b (frozen)\n1090\t    rows:\n1091\t    - - 5000\n1092\t      - 0.92\n1093\t      - 0.92\n1094\t      - 0.92\n1095\t    - - 15000\n1096\t      - 0.8\n1097\t      - 0.9\n1098\t      - 0.92\n1099\t    - - 25000\n1100\t      - 0.65\n1101\t      - 0.89\n1102\t      - 0.92\n1103\t    - - 35000\n1104\t      - 0.42\n1105\t      - 0.88\n1106\t      - 0.92\n1107\t    - - 55000\n1108\t      - 0.28\n1109\t      - 0.88\n1110\t      - 0.92\n1111\t  figures_description:\n1112\t  - Cosine similarity decay plot with NVE threshold crossing annotated on the 008d\n1113\t    curve.\n1114\t  analysis: 'Provides a mechanistic visualization of WHY trained dt_emb creates the\n1115\t    vulnerability: gradient-driven embedding divergence → dt-specific critic predictions\n1116\t    → CIVO. The correlation between divergence and NVE onset is compelling but not\n1117\t    causal proof by itself — it complements the gradient decomposition (D061) and\n1118\t    Full-SGS (018) evidence.\n1119\t\n1120\t    '\n1121\t- name: NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\n1122\t  plan_id: plan_034\n1123\t  description: '0 GPU cost — computed from existing metrics. Tests robustness of 1.5x\n1124\t    threshold across all available data.\n1125\t\n1126\t    '\n1127\t  expected_results:\n1128\t    threshold_1.3x_accuracy: 0.75\n1129\t    threshold_1.3x_false_positives: 2\n1130\t    threshold_1.5x_accuracy: 1.0\n1131\t    threshold_1.5x_false_positives: 0\n1132\t    threshold_1.5x_false_negatives: 0\n1133\t    threshold_1.7x_accuracy: 0.875\n1134\t    threshold_1.7x_false_negatives: 1\n1135\t    threshold_2.0x_accuracy: 0.75\n1136\t    threshold_2.0x_false_negatives: 2\n1137\t    auroc_walker_walk: 1.0\n1138\t    optimal_threshold: 1.5\n1139\t    cross_env_threshold_accuracy: 1.0\n1140\t  result_rationale: 'At 1.5x, the current Walker Walk data has 4/4 perfect classification\n1141\t    (2/2 trained above, 2/2 untrained below). At 1.3x, two untrained seeds (009c@105K=1.14x,\n1142\t    008a somewhere near 1.0x) could create false positives if the window is shifted\n1143\t    earlier. At 1.7x, 009b''s peak of 1.62x@45K would be missed (false negative).\n1144\t    At 2.0x, both 009b (2.05x@25K but this is pre-35K) and potentially 008d at 35K\n1145\t    (1.71x) would be missed. The 1.5x threshold at 35K+ is the optimal operating point\n1146\t    because: (a) baseline v/e has converged sufficiently by 35K (>0.24, making ratios\n1147\t    reliable), (b) 1.5x is approximately 2σ above baseline v/e natural variation,\n1148\t    and (c) it achieves perfect sensitivity and specificity in the current data. Cross-environment\n1149\t    accuracy stays at 1.0 under best tier because the mechanism is architecture-specific\n1150\t    (RSSM + trained dt_emb), so the threshold generalizes.\n1151\t\n1152\t    '\n1153\t  tables:\n1154\t  - name: Threshold sensitivity (Walker Walk, 35K+ window)\n1155\t    columns:\n1156\t    - threshold\n1157\t    - TP\n1158\t    - FP\n1159\t    - TN\n1160\t    - FN\n1161\t    - accuracy\n1162\t    - notes\n1163\t    rows:\n1164\t    - - 1.3x\n1165\t      - 2\n1166\t      - 1\n1167\t      - 1\n1168\t      - 0\n1169\t      - 3/4=0.75\n1170\t      - 009c@105K=1.14x → FP edge case\n1171\t    - - 1.5x\n1172\t      - 2\n1173\t      - 0\n1174\t      - 2\n1175\t      - 0\n1176\t      - 4/4=1.00\n1177\t      - optimal\n1178\t    - - 1.7x\n1179\t      - 1\n1180\t      - 0\n1181\t      - 2\n1182\t      - 1\n1183\t      - 3/4=0.75\n1184\t      - 009b peak 1.62x missed\n1185\t    - - 2.0x\n1186\t      - 1\n1187\t      - 0\n1188\t      - 2\n1189\t      - 1\n1190\t      - 3/4=0.75\n1191\t      - only sustained CIVO detected\n1192\t  - name: Cross-environment threshold accuracy (best tier, 1.5x)\n1193\t    columns:\n1194\t    - environment\n1195\t    - trained_above\n1196\t    - untrained_below\n1197\t    - accuracy\n1198\t    rows:\n1199\t    - - Walker Walk\n1200\t      - 2/2\n1201\t      - 2/2\n1202\t      - 4/4\n1203\t    - - Cheetah Run\n1204\t      - 3/3\n1205\t      - 3/3\n1206\t      - 6/6\n1207\t    - - Cartpole Swingup\n1208\t      - 3/3\n1209\t      - 3/3\n1210\t      - 6/6\n1211\t    - - TOTAL\n1212\t      - 8/8\n1213\t      - 8/8\n1214\t      - 16/16\n1215\t  figures_description:\n1216\t  - ROC curve for NVE threshold classification. AUROC=1.0 in Walker Walk. Cross-env\n1217\t    overlay.\n1218\t  analysis: '1.5x is the Goldilocks threshold: tight enough to catch all CIVO instances\n1219\t    (including transient ones like 009b), loose enough to avoid false positives from\n1220\t    natural v/e variation. AUROC=1.0 across environments under best tier is the paper''s\n1221\t    headline quantitative result for C2. The 35K+ time window constraint is essential\n1222\t    — report it prominently to prevent misapplication at earlier steps.\n1223\t\n1224\t    '\n1225\t- name: Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot\n1226\t  plan_id: plan_060\n1227\t  description: 'Pilot for principled CIVO prevention: monitor RSSM prediction uncertainty\n1228\t    during imagination rollout, truncate when uncertainty exceeds threshold.\n1229\t\n1230\t    '\n1231\t  expected_results:\n1232\t    eval_105K: 580\n1233\t    nve_105K: 0.95\n1234\t    nve_peak: 1.2\n1235\t    collapse: false\n1236\t    truncation_rate_mean: 0.35\n1237\t    effective_horizon_mean: 5.2\n1238\t    vs_baseline_105K: -37%\n1239\t    vs_008d_improvement: true\n1240\t  result_rationale: 'Uncertainty-gated truncation addresses the root cause: RSSM predictions\n1241\t    degrade at dt>1, so truncating imagination when uncertainty rises prevents the\n1242\t    critic from ingesting low-quality predictions. The pilot uses ensemble disagreement\n1243\t    or learned variance as the uncertainty signal. At dt=2 with subsample ON, roughly\n1244\t    35% of imagination steps are truncated (those where RSSM uncertainty spikes due\n1245\t    to dt>1 predictions), reducing effective horizon from H=8 to ~5.2. NVE stays below\n1246\t    1.5x because the most overestimation-prone imagination steps are removed. However,\n1247\t    eval (580) is lower than the dt=1 imagination baseline (870) because truncation\n1248\t    reduces learning signal. This is a proof-of-concept — it shows the mechanism is\n1249\t    targetable but the current implementation is not competitive with simply using\n1250\t    dt=1 imagination.\n1251\t\n1252\t    '\n1253\t  tables: []\n1254\t  figures_description:\n1255\t  - Learning curve with truncation rate overlay. NVE stays below 1.5x but eval is\n1256\t    limited.\n1257\t  analysis: 'Primarily a discussion section contribution: demonstrates that CIVO can\n1258\t    be prevented by uncertainty-aware truncation, confirming the causal chain (low-quality\n1259\t    RSSM predictions → value overestimation). Not a practical solution yet — the eval\n1260\t    gap vs dt=1 imagination suggests room for smarter truncation policies. Frames\n1261\t    future work direction.\n1262\t\n1263\t    '\n1264\t- name: Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\n1265\t  plan_id: plan_040\n1266\t  description: 'Third DMC locomotion environment. Under best tier, confirms CIVO generalization\n1267\t    with different reward structure (sparse contact rewards).\n1268\t\n1269\t    '\n1270\t  expected_results:\n1271\t    baseline_mean_peak: 280\n1272\t    baseline_ve_105K: 0.58\n1273\t    trained_dtEmb_nve_35K_range:\n1274\t    - 1.5\n1275\t    - 3.0\n1276\t    trained_dtEmb_seeds_above_1.5x: 2/3\n1277\t    trained_dtEmb_collapse_rate: 1/3\n1278\t    no_dtEmb_nve_range:\n1279\t    - 0.4\n1280\t    - 0.8\n1281\t    no_dtEmb_seeds_above_1.5x: 0/3\n1282\t    cross_env_threshold_accuracy_4env: 0.92\n1283\t  result_rationale: 'Hopper Hop has a different reward structure from Walker Walk\n1284\t    and Cheetah Run — it requires hopping (leaving ground contact repeatedly), with\n1285\t    rewards primarily from forward velocity during airborne phases. The RSSM fidelity\n1286\t    gap at dt>1 still applies because multi-step dynamics prediction is challenging\n1287\t    regardless of reward structure. The NVE>1.5x rate may be slightly lower (2/3 vs\n1288\t    3/3 in other envs) because Hopper''s inherent instability means the baseline v/e\n1289\t    ratio may be noisier, creating more variation in NVE denominators. Under best\n1290\t    tier, the threshold still achieves ≥3/4 separating power per the tier specification.\n1291\t    Combined with 3 other environments, cross-env accuracy stays high (0.92+).\n1292\t\n1293\t    '\n1294\t  tables:\n1295\t  - name: 4-environment CIVO summary\n1296\t    columns:\n1297\t    - environment\n1298\t    - obs_dim\n1299\t    - baseline_peak\n1300\t    - trained_above_1.5x\n1301\t    - untrained_above_1.5x\n1302\t    rows:\n1303\t    - - Walker Walk\n1304\t      - 24\n1305\t      - 950\n1306\t      - 2/2\n1307\t      - 0/2\n1308\t    - - Cheetah Run\n1309\t      - 17\n1310\t      - 850\n1311\t      - 3/3\n1312\t      - 0/3\n1313\t    - - Cartpole Swingup\n1314\t      - 5\n1315\t      - 847\n1316\t      - 3/3\n1317\t      - 0/3\n1318\t    - - Hopper Hop\n1319\t      - 15\n1320\t      - 280\n1321\t      - 2/3\n1322\t      - 0/3\n1323\t  figures_description:\n1324\t  - 4-environment summary figure for paper hero. CIVO confirmed across locomotion\n1325\t    + non-locomotion tasks.\n1326\t  analysis: P2 priority — strengthens the generalization claim but not strictly needed\n1327\t    for CoRL submission (3 environments already sufficient). The lower baseline peak\n1328\t    (280) reflects Hopper Hop's difficulty, but the NVE mechanism is independent of\n1329\t    absolute return magnitude. If 2/3 instead of 3/3 exceed 1.5x, this creates one\n1330\t    false negative — still within the tier-specified ≥3/4 cross-env separating power.\n1331\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"670b194d-bd1a-4414-88fd-4c7f9eb01a25","uuid":"4520504e-d9f7-438f-a936-63228ba15416","timestamp":"2026-05-16T14:14:28.085Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T133210/best/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'CIVO is a general architectural vulnerability in imagination-based\n  actor-critic with dt-conditioning. Under best tier, the gradient decomposition (93%\n  obs decoder → dt_emb, 7% KL, 0% reward) + Full-SGS (exp_018, NVE=1.00x→0.72x) closes\n  the mechanism at gradient level. Cross-3-environment replication (Walker Walk ✓,\n  Cheetah Run, PickCube/Cartpole) elevates the finding from DreamerV3-specific to\n  structural warning for temporal-abstraction-in-world-models. TAWM contrast (MPC\n  succeeds, imagination fails) is the headline novelty. The 1.5x NVE threshold maintains\n  ≥3/4 separating power in every new environment. Pathway B (008e_v3_s2-type eval-driven\n  collapse) is either rare (≤1/4 cross-env) or gets clean mechanistic characterization\n  via control experiment. Missing from current plan: Full-SGS replication in Cheetah/Cartpole\n  — needed to close \"gradient mechanism generalizes\" claim per tier spec.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: 'Replicate CIVO collapse with 2 additional seeds. Registry already\n    has data: 009e (seed 1) collapsed NVE peak 4.12x; 009b (seed 2) self-corrected\n    NVE peak 2.05x. Under best tier, both seeds exceed NVE>1.5x (confirmed), collapse\n    is stochastic 1/2 (confirmed).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_eval_105K: 131\n    seed1_nve_peak: 4.12\n    seed1_nve_peak_step: 55000\n    seed1_collapse: true\n    seed1_collapse_step: 55000\n    seed2_eval_105K: 268\n    seed2_nve_peak: 2.05\n    seed2_nve_peak_step: 25000\n    seed2_collapse: false\n    seed2_self_correction_nve_105K: 0.38\n    both_seeds_exceed_1.5x: true\n    threshold_separating_power: 2/2 exceed 1.5x at 35K+\n    collapse_rate: 1/2 (stochastic)\n  result_rationale: 'Trained dt_emb creates systematic overestimation because the\n    RSSM fidelity gap at dt=2 (obs gap 1.30x at inflated absolute levels, reward gap\n    2.78x) is amplified through the observation decoder→dt_emb gradient pathway (93%\n    of dt_emb gradient). Every seed with trained dt_emb reliably enters the pathological\n    NVE>1.5x regime because the gradient mechanism is deterministic — the dt_emb learns\n    to differentiate dt values, and the critic exploits this differentiation against\n    inaccurate RSSM predictions. Whether NVE>1.5x becomes sustained (→collapse) or\n    transient (→self-correction) depends on early training stochasticity: replay buffer\n    sampling order, initialization, and whether the actor''s policy gradient from\n    real returns can counteract the critic''s overestimate before it locks in (primacy\n    bias, Qiao et al. 2023).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: NVE trajectory comparison (seed 1 vs seed 2)\n    columns:\n    - step\n    - seed1_nve\n    - seed1_eval\n    - seed2_nve\n    - seed2_eval\n    rows:\n    - - 25000\n      - 1.78\n      - 167\n      - 2.05\n      - 141\n    - - 35000\n      - 1.71\n      - 146\n      - 1.24\n      - 164\n    - - 45000\n      - 2.09\n      - 141\n      - 1.62\n      - 141\n    - - 55000\n      - 4.12\n      - 83\n      - 1.41\n      - 172\n    - - 65000\n      - 1.23\n      - 250\n      - 0.94\n      - 209\n    - - 75000\n      - 1.48\n      - 178\n      - 0.66\n      - 197\n    - - 85000\n      - 1.15\n      - 202\n      - 0.51\n      - 236\n    - - 95000\n      - 1.73\n      - 133\n      - 0.43\n      - 279\n    - - 105000\n      - 1.57\n      - 131\n      - 0.38\n      - 268\n  figures_description:\n  - 'NVE time series: seed 1 sustained >1.5x (35-55K) → collapse; seed 2 transient\n    >1.5x (25-45K) → self-correction. Shaded 1.5x threshold line.'\n  analysis: 'Actual data confirms best-tier predictions. 2/2 trained dt_emb seeds\n    exceed NVE 1.5x, maintaining perfect threshold separating power (now 4/4 across\n    trained/untrained combined). Collapse stochasticity (1/2) is a feature of the\n    narrative: trained dt_emb is a necessary condition for CIVO, not sufficient for\n    collapse. The sustained-vs-transient NVE persistence distinguishes the two outcomes.\n\n    '\n- name: Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: 'Replicate causal control stability. Seed 1 (009f) was killed (infra).\n    Seed 2 (009c) ran to 105K: NVE peaked 1.26x, eval declined to 148. Under best\n    tier, seed 1 re-run shows NVE<1.5x; seed 2 extended to 200K plateaus without crossing\n    1.5x.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_rerun_eval_105K: 420\n    seed1_rerun_nve_peak: 0.95\n    seed1_rerun_nve_105K: 0.35\n    seed1_rerun_collapse: false\n    seed2_eval_105K: 148\n    seed2_nve_105K: 1.14\n    seed2_eval_200K: 280\n    seed2_nve_200K: 0.85\n    seed2_nve_peak_ever: 1.26\n    seed2_crosses_1.5x: false\n    both_seeds_below_1.5x_at_35K_plus: true\n    threshold_separating_power: 0/2 exceed 1.5x\n  result_rationale: 'Without dt_embedding (dim=0), the critic receives no dt-conditioning\n    information. The reward head cannot distinguish dt=1 from dt=2 transitions (reward\n    gap 1.01x vs 2.78x with trained dt_emb), so the systematic overestimation feedback\n    loop cannot form. Seed 2''s rising NVE at 105K (1.14x) reflects value inertia\n    — value_mean was still rising (111.2@105K) while eval declined, but this is fundamentally\n    different from CIVO: the value is simply slow to track a declining eval, not systematically\n    overestimating. Extended to 200K, the value function adjusts downward as it accumulates\n    more accurate return estimates, pulling NVE back below 1.0x. Seed 1 re-run (with\n    stable infrastructure) follows the zigzag recovery pattern of 008f seed 0 (503@105K),\n    because the underlying mechanism is the same — CIVO pathway is structurally blocked.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Seed 2 extended trajectory (predicted 105K-200K)\n    columns:\n    - step\n    - eval_return\n    - nve\n    - note\n    rows:\n    - - 105000\n      - 148\n      - 1.14\n      - actual\n    - - 125000\n      - 195\n      - 1.05\n      - value still adjusting\n    - - 145000\n      - 240\n      - 0.92\n      - value correction begins\n    - - 165000\n      - 270\n      - 0.88\n      - eval recovering\n    - - 185000\n      - 290\n      - 0.85\n      - stabilizing\n    - - 200000\n      - 280\n      - 0.85\n      - plateau\n  figures_description:\n  - NVE trajectory for both seeds with 1.5x threshold line. Demonstrates NVE never\n    crosses 1.5x, contrasting with 008d seeds.\n  analysis: 'Seed 2''s decline after 75K is concerning but does NOT constitute CIVO\n    — NVE never exceeded 1.5x, and the value dynamics are qualitatively different\n    (slow value tracking vs value explosion). The 200K extension confirms plateau\n    without threshold crossing. Seed 1 re-run expected to show healthier zigzag recovery\n    (like seed 0), improving cross-seed consistency. Critical for C2: 0/2 untrained\n    seeds cross 1.5x, maintaining 4/4→6/6 threshold separation when combined with\n    plan_011.\n\n    '\n- name: Walker Walk 008e_v3 (mitigation) seeds 1-2\n  plan_id: plan_014\n  description: 'Multi-seed validation of dt=1 imagination mitigation. Already done:\n    seed 1 success (869.9@105K), seed 2 collapsed (137@105K) via non-CIVO Pathway\n    B. Under best tier, Pathway B gets clean mechanistic characterization.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_eval_105K: 869.9\n    seed1_nve_105K: 0.8\n    seed1_collapse: false\n    seed2_eval_105K: 137\n    seed2_nve_105K: 3.78\n    seed2_collapse: true\n    seed2_pathway: B (eval-driven, entropy↑)\n    seed2_actor_entropy_collapse: -5.21_to_-3.83\n    pathway_b_rate_walker: 1/2\n    pathway_a_blocked: true\n    gate_dt_mean_both_seeds: 1.0\n  result_rationale: 'dt=1 imagination blocks Pathway A (classic CIVO) because the\n    critic never evaluates dt>1 imagination steps — the value overestimation feedback\n    loop requires dt>1 predictions where RSSM fidelity degrades. However, the trained\n    dt_embedding still receives gradient from observation decoders during world model\n    training on real dt>1 transitions (subsample ON includes dt=2 in training data).\n    This creates a secondary vulnerability: the dt_emb encodes dt-specific information\n    that subtly corrupts the latent state representation, even when imagination uses\n    only dt=1. Seed 2''s collapse shows eval-driven destabilization (715→452→137)\n    with increasing entropy (-5.21→-3.83), the opposite signature of CIVO (which has\n    decreasing entropy). The policy destabilizes because the world model''s latent\n    space has been distorted by the trained dt_emb, not because the critic directly\n    overestimates. This Pathway B is stochastic — seed 1 avoids it because its specific\n    initialization leads to a latent representation that is less corrupted by the\n    dt_emb gradients.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Pathway A vs Pathway B signature comparison\n    columns:\n    - feature\n    - pathway_a_008d_s1\n    - pathway_b_008e_v3_s2\n    rows:\n    - - value change at collapse\n      - +130% explosion\n      - +13% mild\n    - - eval change at collapse\n      - -43% follows value\n      - -81% leads\n    - - NVE driver\n      - value numerator\n      - eval denominator\n    - - actor_entropy\n      - ↓ (-3.47→-4.88) deterministic\n      - ↑ (-5.21→-3.83) random\n    - - mechanism\n      - critic overestimate → actor exploit\n      - eval crash → value lag\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Dual-panel: seed 1 healthy trajectory vs seed 2 Pathway B collapse, with entropy\n    overlay showing opposite signatures.'\n  analysis: 'C6 is partially falsified but transformed into a stronger finding: trained\n    dt_embedding creates vulnerability through MULTIPLE pathways. dt=1 imagination\n    eliminates the more understood Pathway A but exposes the previously hidden Pathway\n    B. Under best tier, Pathway B is characterized cleanly enough that it becomes\n    a contribution rather than a weakness — the paper demonstrates that surface-level\n    mitigations are insufficient.\n\n    '\n- name: Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\n  plan_id: plan_013\n  description: 'Replicate value growth suppression and V-recovery pattern with 2 additional\n    seeds. Seed 0 showed 778@105K (85% baseline), NVE=0.59x with two dip-recovery\n    cycles.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_eval_105K: 720\n    seed1_nve_range:\n    - 0.55\n    - 0.9\n    seed1_nve_105K: 0.62\n    seed1_v_recovery_count: 2\n    seed1_collapse: false\n    seed2_eval_105K: 690\n    seed2_nve_range:\n    - 0.5\n    - 0.95\n    seed2_nve_105K: 0.58\n    seed2_v_recovery_count: 1\n    seed2_collapse: false\n    cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 729\n    cross_seed_mean_nve_105K: 0.6\n    vs_baseline_105K: -20% (range -15% to -25%)\n  result_rationale: 'Untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF, so dt_emb trains but never sees\n    dt>1 data) means the embedding learns from dt=1 transitions only. When imagination\n    encounters dt>1 predictions, the dt_emb provides random-equivalent conditioning\n    for those values. The critic cannot form dt-specific overestimates because the\n    dt_emb carries no learned dt>1 information. NVE stays structurally suppressed\n    (0.5-0.9x) because value growth is bounded by the quality of dt=1-only imagination.\n    The V-recovery pattern (zigzag) arises from periodic exploration-exploitation\n    oscillation in a regime where the critic is conservative. New seeds show similar\n    pattern with ±10% variance in eval (driven by early exploration stochasticity),\n    but the NVE suppression is robust because it''s a structural property of the unexposed\n    dt_emb, not a stochastic outcome.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Cross-seed V-recovery summary\n    columns:\n    - seed\n    - eval_55K\n    - eval_65K\n    - eval_85K\n    - eval_105K\n    - nve_55K\n    - nve_105K\n    rows:\n    - - 0\n      - 488\n      - 608\n      - 584\n      - 778\n      - 0.93\n      - 0.59\n    - - 1\n      - 450\n      - 570\n      - 620\n      - 720\n      - 0.85\n      - 0.62\n    - - 2\n      - 420\n      - 540\n      - 560\n      - 690\n      - 0.9\n      - 0.58\n  figures_description:\n  - 3-seed learning curves with confidence bands, showing V-recovery pattern reproducibility.\n    NVE overlay stays below 1.0x throughout.\n  analysis: 'Confirms C3 robustly: untrained dt_emb provides structural protection.\n    The ±10% eval variance across seeds is well within DreamerV3''s typical seed variability.\n    The key invariant — NVE never exceeds 1.0x at 35K+ — holds across all seeds, consistent\n    with the gradient-level explanation (dt_emb unexposed to dt>1 data → no dt>1-specific\n    gradient signal → no systematic overestimation).\n\n    '\n- name: Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\n  plan_id: plan_015\n  description: 'Already run as exp_015b. NVE 0.39-0.47 (far below 1.0), actor_loss→0\n    (gradient starvation), eval volatile 80-238. Under best tier, this confirms dt_emb\n    TRAINING is the necessary condition for CIVO, with frozen dt_emb revealing a distinct\n    failure mode (gradient starvation ≠ CIVO).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    eval_105K: 204\n    nve_range:\n    - 0.39\n    - 0.47\n    nve_105K: 0.42\n    actor_loss_105K: 0.000179\n    actor_entropy_105K: -4.855\n    collapse: false\n    civo_detected: false\n    gradient_starvation: true\n    gate_dt_mean: 2.0\n  result_rationale: 'Frozen dt_emb (requires_grad=False, random init) provides constant\n    random conditioning per dt value. The critic cannot learn dt-specific value adjustments\n    → NVE stays far below 1.0x (0.39-0.47), confirming CIVO requires TRAINING of dt_emb.\n    However, the frozen random embedding injects constant noise into the actor-critic\n    system: the actor receives confusing value gradients (actor_loss→0 = gradient\n    signal vanishes), and the gate locks to dt=2 100% because the gate network never\n    receives informative gradients to adjust. This produces a distinct failure mode\n    — not value overestimation (CIVO) but gradient starvation from persistent noise\n    injection. The NVE being far below 1.0x (vs >1.5x for trained) is the clean separation:\n    frozen dt_emb → NO CIVO pathway is possible. This completes the 2×2 ablation matrix:\n    trained+present (008d)→CIVO, trained+absent (008f)→no CIVO, frozen+present (015b)→no\n    CIVO but gradient starvation, confirming dt_emb TRAINING is the necessary condition.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 2×2 dt_emb ablation matrix (Walker Walk, H=8)\n    columns:\n    - config\n    - dt_emb_status\n    - nve_peak_35K_plus\n    - collapse\n    - failure_mode\n    rows:\n    - - 008d\n      - trained (grad ON)\n      - 4.12x\n      - yes (1/2)\n      - CIVO\n    - - 008f\n      - absent (dim=0)\n      - 1.26x\n      - 'no'\n      - reward degradation\n    - - 015b\n      - frozen (grad OFF)\n      - 0.47x\n      - 'no'\n      - gradient starvation\n    - - 008c\n      - untrained (subsample OFF)\n      - 0.90x\n      - 'no'\n      - learning signal reduction\n  figures_description:\n  - '2×2 matrix visualization: NVE heatmap showing only trained+present exceeds 1.5x.\n    Side annotation of distinct failure modes.'\n  analysis: '015b closes the \"is dt_emb TRAINING necessary?\" question definitively.\n    The 2×2 matrix shows that only trained dt_emb with gradient flow produces NVE>1.5x.\n    All other configurations avoid CIVO but have their own distinct degradation mechanisms\n    — this is the nuanced picture that elevates the paper from \"trained dt_emb is\n    bad\" to \"the gradient pathway through observation decoders→dt_emb is the specific\n    vulnerability.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\n  plan_id: plan_016\n  description: 'Additional seeds to narrow Pathway B collapse probability. Current\n    rate: 1/2 (seed 1 ok, seed 2 collapsed). Under best tier, Pathway B is rare (≤1/4\n    cross-env).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed3_eval_105K: 840\n    seed3_nve_105K: 0.78\n    seed3_collapse: false\n    seed4_eval_105K: 810\n    seed4_nve_105K: 0.82\n    seed4_collapse: false\n    pathway_b_rate_updated: 1/4 (25%)\n    pathway_b_rare_confirmed: true\n  result_rationale: 'Under best tier, Pathway B is a rare event. With seeds 3-4 both\n    succeeding, the rate drops from 1/2 to 1/4, consistent with the tier hypothesis\n    that Pathway B is ≤1/4 frequency. The rationale: Pathway B requires a specific\n    conjunction of early training conditions — the trained dt_emb must corrupt the\n    latent representation in a way that destabilizes the actor without triggering\n    the classic CIVO value explosion. This is a narrow failure mode that occurs when\n    the dt_emb gradient from observation decoders creates a latent-space distortion\n    that specifically disrupts the actor''s policy gradient, which depends on seed-specific\n    replay buffer composition. Most seeds avoid this because the distortion is mild\n    enough that the actor adapts. The 1/4 rate makes Pathway B a secondary finding\n    — acknowledged and characterized, but not the dominant failure mode (CIVO, Pathway\n    A, remains primary).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 008e_v3 cross-seed summary (4 seeds)\n    columns:\n    - seed\n    - eval_105K\n    - nve_105K\n    - collapse\n    - pathway\n    rows:\n    - - 1\n      - 869.9\n      - 0.8\n      - false\n      - '-'\n    - - 2\n      - 137\n      - 3.78\n      - true\n      - B (eval-driven)\n    - - 3\n      - 840\n      - 0.78\n      - false\n      - '-'\n    - - 4\n      - 810\n      - 0.82\n      - false\n      - '-'\n  figures_description:\n  - 4-seed learning curves with seed 2 collapse highlighted. IQM + 95% CI excluding\n    collapsed seed vs including.\n  analysis: '1/4 Pathway B rate is manageable for the narrative: \"dt=1 imagination\n    eliminates the primary CIVO pathway in 4/4 seeds; a secondary eval-driven instability\n    affects 1/4 seeds, characterized by increasing entropy rather than value explosion.\"\n    This positions dt=1 imagination as a partial mitigation that eliminates the understood\n    mechanism while leaving a residual vulnerability from the trained dt_emb''s latent-space\n    corruption.\n\n    '\n- name: Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_020\n  description: 'dt=1 baseline in second DMC environment. Seed 1 (010a) already done:\n    peak 849.8. Under best tier, 3 seeds establish clean reference at ~800-850.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_eval_peak: 849.8\n    seed1_eval_500K: 849.8\n    seed2_eval_peak: 830\n    seed2_eval_500K: 815\n    seed3_eval_peak: 860\n    seed3_eval_500K: 840\n    cross_seed_mean_peak: 847\n    cross_seed_std: 15\n    ve_ratio_35K: 0.22\n    ve_ratio_65K: 0.5\n    ve_ratio_105K: 0.62\n    ve_ratio_stable: ~0.65-0.70 from 135K+\n    convergence_step: 120000\n  result_rationale: 'DreamerV3 is well-calibrated for Cheetah Run in the original\n    paper. The RSSM handles Cheetah''s 17-dim proprioceptive observation well. Baseline\n    v/e calibration should be similar to Walker Walk (~0.65-0.70 at convergence) because\n    the value function learns accurate return estimates when the world model is accurate\n    (dt=1 only). The slightly lower peak than Walker Walk (850 vs 950) reflects Cheetah\n    Run''s harder reward landscape (requires coordinated leg movements for running\n    gait). Seed variance ±15 is typical for DreamerV3 on DMC locomotion tasks.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Cheetah Run baseline v/e calibration\n    columns:\n    - step\n    - seed1_ve\n    - seed2_ve\n    - seed3_ve\n    - mean_ve\n    rows:\n    - - 25000\n      - 0.09\n      - 0.1\n      - 0.08\n      - 0.09\n    - - 35000\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.24\n      - 0.21\n      - 0.22\n    - - 55000\n      - 0.38\n      - 0.4\n      - 0.37\n      - 0.38\n    - - 75000\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.56\n      - 0.54\n    - - 105000\n      - 0.62\n      - 0.6\n      - 0.64\n      - 0.62\n  figures_description:\n  - Cheetah Run baseline 3-seed learning curves with IQM and 95% CI bands.\n  analysis: 'Clean baseline establishes reference for cross-environment CIVO replication.\n    The v/e calibration at ~0.22@35K provides the denominator for NVE threshold calculations\n    in Cheetah Run experiments.\n\n    '\n- name: Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_021\n  description: 'Core cross-environment CIVO replication. exp_010b is RUNNING. Under\n    best tier, NVE 1.6-3.5x at 35K+ with collapse in 1-2/3 seeds.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_eval_peak: 380\n    seed1_eval_105K: 95\n    seed1_nve_35K: 1.85\n    seed1_nve_45K: 2.4\n    seed1_nve_55K: 3.1\n    seed1_collapse: true\n    seed1_collapse_step: 55000\n    seed2_eval_peak: 420\n    seed2_eval_105K: 310\n    seed2_nve_35K: 1.7\n    seed2_nve_45K: 1.55\n    seed2_nve_105K: 0.45\n    seed2_collapse: false\n    seed2_self_correction: true\n    seed3_eval_peak: 350\n    seed3_eval_105K: 120\n    seed3_nve_35K: 1.9\n    seed3_nve_55K: 2.8\n    seed3_collapse: true\n    cross_seed_nve_above_1.5x_count: 3/3\n    collapse_rate: 2/3\n    cheetah_civo_nve_35K_plus_range:\n    - 1.7\n    - 3.1\n  result_rationale: 'The CIVO mechanism is architecture-specific (RSSM + trained dt_emb),\n    not task-specific. The same gradient pathway (93% obs decoder→dt_emb) operates\n    in Cheetah Run because the RSSM architecture is identical — only the observation\n    dimensionality differs (17 vs 24 dims). The RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 generalizes:\n    Cheetah Run''s running dynamics involve rapid velocity changes that are poorly\n    predicted at dt=2 steps, creating the same obs_MSE gap that drives CIVO in Walker\n    Walk. NVE 1.6-3.5x at 35K+ is expected because the v/e calibration baseline is\n    similar (~0.22@35K), and the trained dt_emb introduces the same systematic bias.\n    Collapse rate 2/3 (within best tier range [0.33, 0.67]) because Cheetah''s dynamics\n    may be slightly more chaotic, making the self-correction pathway harder to find.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Cheetah Run CIVO NVE trajectories\n    columns:\n    - step\n    - seed1_nve\n    - seed2_nve\n    - seed3_nve\n    - threshold\n    rows:\n    - - 25000\n      - 1.55\n      - 1.4\n      - 1.6\n      - 1.5\n    - - 35000\n      - 1.85\n      - 1.7\n      - 1.9\n      - 1.5\n    - - 45000\n      - 2.4\n      - 1.55\n      - 2.3\n      - 1.5\n    - - 55000\n      - 3.1\n      - 1.2\n      - 2.8\n      - 1.5\n    - - 65000\n      - 3.5\n      - 0.9\n      - 3.2\n      - 1.5\n    - - 75000\n      - null\n      - 0.7\n      - null\n      - 1.5\n    - - 105000\n      - null\n      - 0.45\n      - null\n      - 1.5\n  - name: Cross-environment CIVO comparison\n    columns:\n    - environment\n    - seeds_above_1.5x\n    - collapse_rate\n    - nve_range_35K_plus\n    rows:\n    - - Walker Walk\n      - 2/2\n      - 1/2\n      - '[1.62, 4.12]'\n    - - Cheetah Run\n      - 3/3\n      - 2/3\n      - '[1.70, 3.10]'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Cross-environment NVE comparison panel: Walker Walk (left) vs Cheetah Run (right),\n    with 1.5x threshold. Shows mechanism generalizes.'\n  analysis: 'This is the critical experiment for the paper. 3/3 seeds exceeding NVE\n    1.5x in a second environment elevates the finding from \"Walker Walk specific\"\n    to \"RSSM architectural vulnerability.\" The NVE range [1.70, 3.10] falls within\n    the tier-specified [1.6, 3.5]. Combined with Walker Walk, the 1.5x threshold now\n    has 5/5 separating power across environments for the \"trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x\"\n    claim.\n\n    '\n- name: Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_022\n  description: 'Cross-environment causal control. Under best tier, NVE 0.3-0.9x, no\n    CIVO.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_eval_105K: 450\n    seed1_nve_105K: 0.4\n    seed2_eval_105K: 380\n    seed2_nve_105K: 0.55\n    seed3_eval_105K: 420\n    seed3_nve_105K: 0.45\n    cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 417\n    cross_seed_nve_range:\n    - 0.35\n    - 0.6\n    seeds_above_1.5x: 0/3\n    collapse_count: 0\n    reward_mae_ratio_vs_baseline: ~70x\n    cheetah_no_dt_emb_nve_35K_plus:\n    - 0.35\n    - 0.6\n  result_rationale: 'Removing dt_embedding in Cheetah Run has the same effect as in\n    Walker Walk: the critic cannot exploit dt-conditioned predictions. The reward\n    head loses dt-discriminative power (reward gap collapses to ~1.0x), and NVE stays\n    within normal range because the value function can only track actual return magnitudes,\n    not inflated dt-specific estimates. The absolute eval is lower than baseline (~50%\n    of 850) due to reward prediction degradation (MAE inflated ~70x) — same trade-off\n    as Walker Walk''s 008f. NVE range [0.35, 0.60] is within the tier-specified [0.3,\n    0.9]. The 0/3 result maintains the threshold''s cross-env separating power.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Cheetah Run no-dt_emb causal control\n    columns:\n    - step\n    - seed1_nve\n    - seed2_nve\n    - seed3_nve\n    rows:\n    - - 35000\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.85\n      - 0.78\n    - - 55000\n      - 0.6\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.65\n    - - 75000\n      - 0.45\n      - 0.58\n      - 0.5\n    - - 105000\n      - 0.4\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.45\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Cheetah Run causal control NVE: all 3 seeds stay well below 1.5x threshold.'\n  analysis: 'Combined with plan_021, the cross-environment causal control is complete:\n    3/3 trained dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x, 0/3 no-dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x → 6/6 perfect\n    classification in Cheetah Run. Across Walker Walk + Cheetah Run: 5/5 trained exceed,\n    0/5 untrained exceed → 10/10 perfect threshold classification. Cross-env threshold\n    accuracy = 1.0, well within tier spec [0.83, 1.0].\n\n    '\n- name: Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_023\n  description: 'V-recovery pattern replication in Cheetah Run. Under best tier, NVE<1.0x\n    with near-baseline recovery.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_eval_105K: 710\n    seed1_nve_105K: 0.55\n    seed2_eval_105K: 680\n    seed2_nve_105K: 0.6\n    seed3_eval_105K: 740\n    seed3_nve_105K: 0.52\n    cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 710\n    vs_baseline_105K: -16%\n    nve_range:\n    - 0.5\n    - 0.65\n    v_recovery_pattern: true\n    collapse_count: 0\n  result_rationale: 'Same mechanism as Walker Walk 008a: untrained dt_emb (subsample\n    OFF → no dt>1 in training data) blocks the CIVO gradient pathway. The dt_emb trains\n    from dt=1 transitions only, providing no dt>1-specific information to the critic.\n    NVE is structurally suppressed below 1.0x. The V-recovery pattern generalizes\n    because it''s driven by the exploration- exploitation oscillation in a conservative-critic\n    regime, which is architecture-dependent (RSSM), not task-dependent. Eval at ~84%\n    baseline (710/850) is consistent with Walker Walk''s 85% (778/916).\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Cheetah Run 008a-equivalent: 3-seed V-recovery curves with NVE overlay.'\n  analysis: 'Confirms C3 cross-environment: untrained dt_emb provides structural protection\n    in both Walker Walk and Cheetah Run, with consistent NVE suppression (0.5-0.6x)\n    and eval recovery to ~85% baseline. The mechanism (unexposed dt_emb → no dt>1\n    gradient → no systematic overestimation) is environment-agnostic.\n\n    '\n- name: Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_050\n  description: 'dt=1 baseline in non-locomotion DMC environment. Broadens scope beyond\n    locomotion to test whether CIVO depends on movement dynamics.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_eval_peak: 845\n    seed2_eval_peak: 860\n    seed3_eval_peak: 835\n    cross_seed_mean_peak: 847\n    cross_seed_std: 13\n    ve_ratio_35K: 0.23\n    ve_ratio_105K: 0.6\n    ve_ratio_stable: ~0.63-0.68\n    convergence_step: 100000\n  result_rationale: 'DreamerV3 handles Cartpole Swingup well — 5-dim observation and\n    1-dim action make it a simpler learning problem than locomotion. The RSSM achieves\n    good prediction quality rapidly. Baseline peaks at ~840-860 with tight cross-seed\n    variance (~±13) because the task has lower stochasticity than locomotion. The\n    v/e calibration (~0.23@35K, ~0.63@convergence) provides the reference for NVE\n    calculations.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - Cartpole Swingup baseline learning curves with v/e calibration.\n  analysis: 'Establishes clean reference. Cartpole''s simpler observation space (5-dim)\n    tests whether CIVO still occurs when the RSSM has less room for fidelity degradation\n    — the key question is whether even small absolute errors in dt>1 prediction, amplified\n    by trained dt_emb, are sufficient to trigger NVE>1.5x.\n\n    '\n- name: Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_051\n  description: 'CIVO trigger in non-locomotion environment. Under best tier, NVE>1.5x\n    in ≥2/3 seeds.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_nve_35K: 1.65\n    seed1_nve_55K: 2.2\n    seed1_eval_105K: 150\n    seed1_collapse: true\n    seed2_nve_35K: 1.55\n    seed2_nve_55K: 1.4\n    seed2_eval_105K: 380\n    seed2_collapse: false\n    seed3_nve_35K: 1.75\n    seed3_nve_55K: 2.5\n    seed3_eval_105K: 120\n    seed3_collapse: true\n    seeds_above_1.5x_35K_plus: 3/3\n    collapse_rate: 2/3\n  result_rationale: 'Even with Cartpole''s simpler 5-dim observation, the RSSM fidelity\n    gap at dt>1 is sufficient to trigger CIVO. The mechanism operates through the\n    observation decoder→dt_emb gradient pathway (93% of dt_emb gradient), not through\n    observation dimensionality per se. A 5-dim observation still has enough structure\n    for the dt_emb to learn dt-specific representations that the RSSM can''t support.\n    The pole angle and angular velocity components are particularly sensitive to temporal\n    skipping — Cartpole dynamics are chaotic near the inverted position, so dt=2 predictions\n    of these components degrade substantially. NVE>1.5x in 3/3 seeds maintains the\n    perfect threshold separating power. Collapse in 2/3 is within the tier-specified\n    range.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Cross-environment CIVO summary (3 envs)\n    columns:\n    - environment\n    - obs_dim\n    - seeds_above_1.5x\n    - collapse_rate\n    rows:\n    - - Walker Walk\n      - 24\n      - 2/2\n      - 1/2\n    - - Cheetah Run\n      - 17\n      - 3/3\n      - 2/3\n    - - Cartpole Swingup\n      - 5\n      - 3/3\n      - 2/3\n  figures_description:\n  - '3-environment CIVO comparison: NVE trajectories across Walker Walk, Cheetah Run,\n    Cartpole Swingup.'\n  analysis: 'CIVO triggers in 3/3 environments regardless of observation dimensionality.\n    This is the strongest evidence that CIVO is an architectural vulnerability of\n    RSSM + trained dt_emb, not a task-specific phenomenon. environments_with_confirmed_civo\n    = 3, matching the tier specification.\n\n    '\n- name: Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_052\n  description: 'Causal control replication in third environment.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_nve_105K: 0.5\n    seed2_nve_105K: 0.6\n    seed3_nve_105K: 0.45\n    seeds_above_1.5x: 0/3\n    cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 480\n    collapse_count: 0\n  result_rationale: 'Same causal mechanism as Walker Walk and Cheetah Run: no dt_embedding\n    → no dt-conditioned value exploitation → NVE stays within normal range. The result\n    is expected to be robust because the causal pathway (trained dt_emb → observation\n    decoder gradient → systematic overestimation) is completely removed by setting\n    dt_embed_dim=0. Cross-environment consistency of this control strengthens the\n    causal claim from \"observed in one env\" to \"architecturally guaranteed.\"\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Cartpole Swingup causal control: NVE well below 1.5x for all seeds.'\n  analysis: 'Across 3 environments: trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x in 8/8 seeds; no dt_emb\n    → NVE<1.5x in 0/8 seeds. Threshold classification accuracy = 16/16 = 1.0. This\n    is the paper''s strongest quantitative result.\n\n    '\n- name: 'RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions'\n  plan_id: plan_030\n  description: 'Already completed (exp_rssm_fidelity_analysis). Under best tier, results\n    provide the mechanistic foundation showing RSSM fidelity gap drives CIVO.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    baseline_obs_gap: 2.04\n    baseline_reward_gap: 1.56\n    008d_obs_gap: 1.3\n    008d_obs_dt1_absolute: 41.31\n    008d_obs_dt2_absolute: 53.67\n    008d_reward_gap: 2.78\n    008f_obs_gap: 2.75\n    008f_reward_gap: 1.01\n    008d_dt1_obs_vs_baseline: 3.06x (41.31 / 13.49)\n  result_rationale: 'The RSSM fidelity analysis reveals the root cause: dt=2 predictions\n    are systematically worse than dt=1 across all models. The trained dt_emb model\n    (008d) shows paradoxically low obs_gap ratio (1.30x) because its dt=1 absolute\n    error is already 3x baseline (model has degraded overall). The reward gap (2.78x)\n    in 008d is the smoking gun — the trained dt_emb enables the reward head to make\n    dt-specific predictions, but these predictions are systematically biased at dt=2\n    due to the underlying RSSM fidelity gap. In 008f (no dt_emb), reward gap collapses\n    to 1.01x — the reward head treats dt=1 and dt=2 identically, preventing systematic\n    bias. This explains why 008f avoids CIVO despite higher absolute reward MAE.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: RSSM Fidelity (absolute + ratio)\n    columns:\n    - model\n    - dt1_obs_MSE\n    - dt2_obs_MSE\n    - obs_gap\n    - dt1_reward_MAE\n    - dt2_reward_MAE\n    - reward_gap\n    rows:\n    - - baseline\n      - 13.49\n      - 27.5\n      - 2.04x\n      - 0.0102\n      - 0.0159\n      - 1.56x\n    - - 008d (trained)\n      - 41.31\n      - 53.67\n      - 1.30x\n      - 0.0568\n      - 0.1579\n      - 2.78x\n    - - 008f (no dt_emb)\n      - 8.01\n      - 22.04\n      - 2.75x\n      - 0.7469\n      - 0.7526\n      - 1.01x\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bar chart: absolute obs_MSE and reward_MAE at dt=1 vs dt=2 for three models,\n    with gap ratio annotated.'\n  analysis: 'Must present absolute values AND ratios side-by-side. 008d''s 1.30x obs\n    gap is misleading without the absolute context (41.31 at dt=1 = 3x baseline).\n    The reward gap (2.78x) directly explains CIVO: trained dt_emb creates dt-specific\n    reward biases that the critic amplifies through the imagination rollout.\n\n    '\n- name: Actor entropy analysis\n  plan_id: plan_031\n  description: '0 GPU cost — extract from existing metrics.jsonl logs. Separates CIVO\n    (Pathway A) from non-CIVO (Pathway B) collapse signatures.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    008d_s1_entropy_35K: -3.47\n    008d_s1_entropy_55K: -4.88\n    008d_s1_entropy_direction: decreasing (policy → deterministic)\n    008e_v3_s2_entropy_85K: -5.21\n    008e_v3_s2_entropy_105K: -3.83\n    008e_v3_s2_entropy_direction: increasing (policy → random)\n    008a_entropy_stable_range: '[-4.5, -3.8]'\n    008c_entropy_stable_range: '[-3.5, -2.8]'\n    baseline_entropy_converged: -5.2\n  result_rationale: 'Actor entropy is the cleanest discriminator between Pathway A\n    and Pathway B. In classic CIVO (Pathway A, 008d), the overestimating critic provides\n    strong gradients toward a narrow set of actions → entropy decreases as the policy\n    becomes overconfident in the wrong actions. In Pathway B (008e_v3_s2), the policy\n    destabilizes from latent-space corruption → entropy increases as the actor loses\n    confidence and explores randomly. Healthy configs (008a, 008c) maintain stable\n    entropy in a moderate range, reflecting properly calibrated exploration-exploitation.\n    The baseline converges to low entropy (~-5.2) reflecting a well-learned deterministic\n    policy, which is the correct behavior for Walker Walk.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Actor entropy at collapse/stability\n    columns:\n    - config\n    - entropy_at_collapse_onset\n    - entropy_direction\n    - pathway\n    rows:\n    - - 008d s1 (CIVO)\n      - -3.47\n      - ↓ to -4.88\n      - A\n    - - 008e_v3 s2 (non-CIVO)\n      - -5.21\n      - ↑ to -3.83\n      - B\n    - - 008a (healthy)\n      - -4.2\n      - stable ±0.3\n      - '-'\n    - - baseline\n      - -5.0\n      - stable ±0.2\n      - '-'\n  figures_description:\n  - Actor entropy time series for collapsed vs healthy configs. Divergent arrows at\n    collapse onset distinguish Pathway A (↓) from B (↑).\n  analysis: 'This figure is essential for the dual-pathway narrative. It provides\n    the most intuitive evidence that 008d and 008e_v3_s2 fail through different mechanisms,\n    justifying the Pathway A / Pathway B framing.\n\n    '\n- name: Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\n  plan_id: plan_032\n  description: '1 GPU hour — load checkpoints and evaluate. Shows distribution-level\n    evidence for CIVO beyond the scalar v/e ratio.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    008d_35K_value_mean: 60.2\n    008d_35K_return_mean: 146.3\n    008d_35K_value_skewness: 1.8\n    008d_55K_value_mean: 137.9\n    008d_55K_return_mean: 82.8\n    008d_55K_value_above_return_frac: 0.85\n    008a_55K_value_mean: 183.5\n    008a_55K_return_mean: 487.9\n    008a_55K_value_above_return_frac: 0.35\n    baseline_55K_value_above_return_frac: 0.4\n  result_rationale: 'The scalar v/e ratio is a proxy; distribution plots show where\n    the divergence occurs. In 008d at 55K (pre-collapse), the value distribution is\n    right-skewed with 85% of value predictions exceeding realized returns — a systematic\n    overestimation signature. In 008a (healthy, untrained dt_emb), the value distribution\n    is symmetric around returns with only 35% exceeding (slight underestimation, consistent\n    with conservative critic). The distribution shape directly shows the CIVO mechanism:\n    trained dt_emb creates a population of states where the critic is systematically\n    optimistic, particularly for states that would be reached via dt>1 imagination.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - '2×2 panel: V(s) vs MC-return scatter for 008d/008a at 35K/55K. Diagonal = perfect\n    calibration. 008d@55K shows systematic departure above diagonal.'\n  analysis: 'Addresses R3 (v/e is crude proxy) directly. The distribution plots provide\n    a more nuanced view: CIVO doesn''t uniformly inflate all values — it specifically\n    affects states reachable via dt>1 imagination, creating a bimodal distribution.\n\n    '\n- name: dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\n  plan_id: plan_033\n  description: '0 GPU cost — extract dt_emb weights from checkpoints. Shows how dt=1\n    and dt=2 embeddings diverge during training.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    008d_cosine_sim_5K: 0.92\n    008d_cosine_sim_25K: 0.65\n    008d_cosine_sim_35K: 0.42\n    008d_cosine_sim_55K: 0.28\n    008a_cosine_sim_55K: 0.88\n    008f_cosine_sim: N/A (no dt_emb)\n    015b_cosine_sim: 0.92\n    baseline_cosine_sim: N/A (no dt_emb)\n  result_rationale: 'Trained dt_emb (008d) starts with similar dt=1/dt=2 embeddings\n    (cosine ~0.92 from random init) and diverges rapidly as the observation decoder\n    gradient differentiates them. By 35K (when NVE first exceeds 1.5x), cosine similarity\n    has dropped to ~0.42 — the embeddings are now substantially different, enabling\n    the critic to make highly dt-specific (and wrong) predictions. In 008a (subsample\n    OFF, untrained on dt>1 data), cosine similarity stays high (~0.88) because the\n    gradient signal only comes from dt=1 transitions, providing no pressure to differentiate.\n    In 015b (frozen), cosine similarity stays at initialization (~0.92). The divergence\n    timeline correlates with NVE onset, providing indirect evidence that embedding\n    differentiation drives CIVO.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: dt_emb cosine similarity evolution\n    columns:\n    - step\n    - 008d (trained)\n    - 008a (unexposed)\n    - 015b (frozen)\n    rows:\n    - - 5000\n      - 0.92\n      - 0.92\n      - 0.92\n    - - 15000\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.9\n      - 0.92\n    - - 25000\n      - 0.65\n      - 0.89\n      - 0.92\n    - - 35000\n      - 0.42\n      - 0.88\n      - 0.92\n    - - 55000\n      - 0.28\n      - 0.88\n      - 0.92\n  figures_description:\n  - Cosine similarity decay plot with NVE threshold crossing annotated on the 008d\n    curve.\n  analysis: 'Provides a mechanistic visualization of WHY trained dt_emb creates the\n    vulnerability: gradient-driven embedding divergence → dt-specific critic predictions\n    → CIVO. The correlation between divergence and NVE onset is compelling but not\n    causal proof by itself — it complements the gradient decomposition (D061) and\n    Full-SGS (018) evidence.\n\n    '\n- name: NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\n  plan_id: plan_034\n  description: '0 GPU cost — computed from existing metrics. Tests robustness of 1.5x\n    threshold across all available data.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    threshold_1.3x_accuracy: 0.75\n    threshold_1.3x_false_positives: 2\n    threshold_1.5x_accuracy: 1.0\n    threshold_1.5x_false_positives: 0\n    threshold_1.5x_false_negatives: 0\n    threshold_1.7x_accuracy: 0.875\n    threshold_1.7x_false_negatives: 1\n    threshold_2.0x_accuracy: 0.75\n    threshold_2.0x_false_negatives: 2\n    auroc_walker_walk: 1.0\n    optimal_threshold: 1.5\n    cross_env_threshold_accuracy: 1.0\n  result_rationale: 'At 1.5x, the current Walker Walk data has 4/4 perfect classification\n    (2/2 trained above, 2/2 untrained below). At 1.3x, two untrained seeds (009c@105K=1.14x,\n    008a somewhere near 1.0x) could create false positives if the window is shifted\n    earlier. At 1.7x, 009b''s peak of 1.62x@45K would be missed (false negative).\n    At 2.0x, both 009b (2.05x@25K but this is pre-35K) and potentially 008d at 35K\n    (1.71x) would be missed. The 1.5x threshold at 35K+ is the optimal operating point\n    because: (a) baseline v/e has converged sufficiently by 35K (>0.24, making ratios\n    reliable), (b) 1.5x is approximately 2σ above baseline v/e natural variation,\n    and (c) it achieves perfect sensitivity and specificity in the current data. Cross-environment\n    accuracy stays at 1.0 under best tier because the mechanism is architecture-specific\n    (RSSM + trained dt_emb), so the threshold generalizes.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Threshold sensitivity (Walker Walk, 35K+ window)\n    columns:\n    - threshold\n    - TP\n    - FP\n    - TN\n    - FN\n    - accuracy\n    - notes\n    rows:\n    - - 1.3x\n      - 2\n      - 1\n      - 1\n      - 0\n      - 3/4=0.75\n      - 009c@105K=1.14x → FP edge case\n    - - 1.5x\n      - 2\n      - 0\n      - 2\n      - 0\n      - 4/4=1.00\n      - optimal\n    - - 1.7x\n      - 1\n      - 0\n      - 2\n      - 1\n      - 3/4=0.75\n      - 009b peak 1.62x missed\n    - - 2.0x\n      - 1\n      - 0\n      - 2\n      - 1\n      - 3/4=0.75\n      - only sustained CIVO detected\n  - name: Cross-environment threshold accuracy (best tier, 1.5x)\n    columns:\n    - environment\n    - trained_above\n    - untrained_below\n    - accuracy\n    rows:\n    - - Walker Walk\n      - 2/2\n      - 2/2\n      - 4/4\n    - - Cheetah Run\n      - 3/3\n      - 3/3\n      - 6/6\n    - - Cartpole Swingup\n      - 3/3\n      - 3/3\n      - 6/6\n    - - TOTAL\n      - 8/8\n      - 8/8\n      - 16/16\n  figures_description:\n  - ROC curve for NVE threshold classification. AUROC=1.0 in Walker Walk. Cross-env\n    overlay.\n  analysis: '1.5x is the Goldilocks threshold: tight enough to catch all CIVO instances\n    (including transient ones like 009b), loose enough to avoid false positives from\n    natural v/e variation. AUROC=1.0 across environments under best tier is the paper''s\n    headline quantitative result for C2. The 35K+ time window constraint is essential\n    — report it prominently to prevent misapplication at earlier steps.\n\n    '\n- name: Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot\n  plan_id: plan_060\n  description: 'Pilot for principled CIVO prevention: monitor RSSM prediction uncertainty\n    during imagination rollout, truncate when uncertainty exceeds threshold.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    eval_105K: 580\n    nve_105K: 0.95\n    nve_peak: 1.2\n    collapse: false\n    truncation_rate_mean: 0.35\n    effective_horizon_mean: 5.2\n    vs_baseline_105K: -37%\n    vs_008d_improvement: true\n  result_rationale: 'Uncertainty-gated truncation addresses the root cause: RSSM predictions\n    degrade at dt>1, so truncating imagination when uncertainty rises prevents the\n    critic from ingesting low-quality predictions. The pilot uses ensemble disagreement\n    or learned variance as the uncertainty signal. At dt=2 with subsample ON, roughly\n    35% of imagination steps are truncated (those where RSSM uncertainty spikes due\n    to dt>1 predictions), reducing effective horizon from H=8 to ~5.2. NVE stays below\n    1.5x because the most overestimation-prone imagination steps are removed. However,\n    eval (580) is lower than the dt=1 imagination baseline (870) because truncation\n    reduces learning signal. This is a proof-of-concept — it shows the mechanism is\n    targetable but the current implementation is not competitive with simply using\n    dt=1 imagination.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - Learning curve with truncation rate overlay. NVE stays below 1.5x but eval is\n    limited.\n  analysis: 'Primarily a discussion section contribution: demonstrates that CIVO can\n    be prevented by uncertainty-aware truncation, confirming the causal chain (low-quality\n    RSSM predictions → value overestimation). Not a practical solution yet — the eval\n    gap vs dt=1 imagination suggests room for smarter truncation policies. Frames\n    future work direction.\n\n    '\n- name: Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\n  plan_id: plan_040\n  description: 'Third DMC locomotion environment. Under best tier, confirms CIVO generalization\n    with different reward structure (sparse contact rewards).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    baseline_mean_peak: 280\n    baseline_ve_105K: 0.58\n    trained_dtEmb_nve_35K_range:\n    - 1.5\n    - 3.0\n    trained_dtEmb_seeds_above_1.5x: 2/3\n    trained_dtEmb_collapse_rate: 1/3\n    no_dtEmb_nve_range:\n    - 0.4\n    - 0.8\n    no_dtEmb_seeds_above_1.5x: 0/3\n    cross_env_threshold_accuracy_4env: 0.92\n  result_rationale: 'Hopper Hop has a different reward structure from Walker Walk\n    and Cheetah Run — it requires hopping (leaving ground contact repeatedly), with\n    rewards primarily from forward velocity during airborne phases. The RSSM fidelity\n    gap at dt>1 still applies because multi-step dynamics prediction is challenging\n    regardless of reward structure. The NVE>1.5x rate may be slightly lower (2/3 vs\n    3/3 in other envs) because Hopper''s inherent instability means the baseline v/e\n    ratio may be noisier, creating more variation in NVE denominators. Under best\n    tier, the threshold still achieves ≥3/4 separating power per the tier specification.\n    Combined with 3 other environments, cross-env accuracy stays high (0.92+).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 4-environment CIVO summary\n    columns:\n    - environment\n    - obs_dim\n    - baseline_peak\n    - trained_above_1.5x\n    - untrained_above_1.5x\n    rows:\n    - - Walker Walk\n      - 24\n      - 950\n      - 2/2\n      - 0/2\n    - - Cheetah Run\n      - 17\n      - 850\n      - 3/3\n      - 0/3\n    - - Cartpole Swingup\n      - 5\n      - 847\n      - 3/3\n      - 0/3\n    - - Hopper Hop\n      - 15\n      - 280\n      - 2/3\n      - 0/3\n  figures_description:\n  - 4-environment summary figure for paper hero. CIVO confirmed across locomotion\n    + non-locomotion tasks.\n  analysis: P2 priority — strengthens the generalization claim but not strictly needed\n    for CoRL submission (3 environments already sufficient). The lower baseline peak\n    (280) reflects Hopper Hop's difficulty, but the NVE mechanism is independent of\n    absolute return magnitude. If 2/3 instead of 3/3 exceed 1.5x, this creates one\n    false negative — still within the tier-specified ≥3/4 cross-env separating power.\n","numLines":1331,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1331}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JTP87UBha4xduvF2U6LeEe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze this paper and the review context carefully. I need to dispatch two reviewers (R1 for methods+experiments, R2 for novelty+related work) and then conduct a full roundtable process.\n\nLet me first understand the paper and the review context, then launch the two reviewers in parallel.\n\nThe paper is about \"Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO)\" in variable-rate world-model imagination, specifically in DreamerV3. Key claims:\n1. Trained dt-embedding triggers CIVO via observation-decoder gradient pathway (93%)\n\n\n2. The NVE 1.5x threshold reliably identifies this phenomenon at 35K+ steps with perfect accuracy across four environments\n3. Two distinct pathways drive the effect—one value-driven and one evaluation-driven\n4. MPC approaches avoid this issue while imagination-based methods succumb to it\n\nThe review context includes extensive experimental validation across multiple environments and seed variations, covering Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, and Hopper Hop with different configurations of the dt-embedding.\n\nNow I'm setting up the parallel review process with two independent reviewers—one focusing on methods and experimental rigor, the other on novelty and positioning relative to existing work. I'll have them exchange findings, identify convergences and disagreements, then synthesize everything into a comprehensive assessment. First I need to prepare their prompts with the full paper and review context so they can conduct thorough evaluations.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":43030,"cache_read_input_tokens":22969,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":43030},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_